WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.990
HOST: So I don't
think Jeff Bezos needs

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.160
much of an introduction.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.090
You've perhaps heard
of this small company

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.600
he runs out of some
city in the west coast--

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:22.457
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:22.457 --> 00:00:23.790
--where there's another company.

00:00:27.446 --> 00:00:31.958
I took a poll of the
faculty recently and--

00:00:31.958 --> 00:00:33.890
I was at a faculty lunch
and we were talking

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.380
about what people did online.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.180
And I sort of ask
people questions

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.221
and eventually I said,
well, how many of you have

00:00:40.221 --> 00:00:42.110
bought something on amazon.com.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:45.182
And it was actually 100%,
I think, raised their hand.

00:00:45.182 --> 00:00:46.146
BEZOS: Yeah, baby!

00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:48.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.820
HOST: I guarantee it
was not one of those.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.830
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:56.480
Any rate, Jeff is a
graduate of Princeton.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.390
And I'm pleased to say, as
he was telling me upstairs,

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.490
he graduated from
Princeton when it was still

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:03.590
an EECS-- an electrical
engineering and computer

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:04.970
science department.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:07.700
Before the department
bifurcated.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.860
And so he does have an
electrical engineering

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.650
and computer science degree.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:16.280
And I think-- he tells me
he's glad he has one of those.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.380
And as he should be.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:22.655
After leaving Princeton,
he went to Bankers Trust.

00:01:22.655 --> 00:01:24.260
He worked there
in the development

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:25.230
of computer systems.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.176
So presumably if you
need help with code,

00:01:28.176 --> 00:01:30.050
he can help you with
some of your programming

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.330
and stuff of that nature.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:34.800
After that he went to D.E. Shaw.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.270
Well known for their
high-tech approach to finance.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:42.080
And in fact, one of two
financial services companies--

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.770
three, I guess-- that belongs
to our industrial program

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:47.540
in the department.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:51.260
And then he left D.E.
Shaw to start amazon.com.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:54.201
And I'm sure he can tell you
more about that than I can.

00:01:54.201 --> 00:01:54.700
Jeff.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:55.408
BEZOS: Thank you.

00:01:55.408 --> 00:01:58.612
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:08.830
Thank you, you guys
it's great to be here.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:11.570
So I'll start out telling you
a little bit about the founding

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:15.140
of amazon.com and then I'm going
to do my favorite thing which

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.900
is to give a live website demo.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.610
This is the house where
Amazon was founded.

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:27.920
On the left-hand side here is
this little enclosed garage.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.030
I was living in New York City,
working for D.E. Shaw and Co

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:37.640
and I came across the fact that
web usage was growing at 2,300%

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:38.250
a year.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.460
This was one of those
weird things where

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.020
you could measure
the rate of growth

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.730
without actually knowing
the baseline usage.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.150
And people were doing that
because they could put sniffers

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.580
at various points--
at various nodes--

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.920
and they could sort of see
how fast the traffic levels

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.390
were growing past those nodes.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:56.390
And they could get a
statistical sample of those

00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:59.570
but they actually had no
idea what baseline usage was.

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:02.090
But you could look
around the web already,

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:05.870
in the spring of 1994, and
see that it was big enough

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:09.090
that if it was growing at
2,300% a year, pretty soon

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.860
it was going to be huge.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:16.010
And so I kind of
packed up my wife and I

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:19.940
and we flew to Fort Worth,
Texas where my dad gave us

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:23.114
a car-- a 1988 Chevy Blazer.

00:03:23.114 --> 00:03:24.530
Which by the way,
Consumer Reports

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.890
recommends not to buy used
under any circumstances

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.680
for any price.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.240
And we drove that car
from Texas to Seattle.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.940
Seattle was chosen
as the best place

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:40.790
to start amazon.com
because it was

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:44.780
a large pool of technical talent
and also nearby at the time,

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.450
was the largest book
warehouse in the world.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.660
In a town called
Roseburg, Oregon.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.390
So we got there and the
first order of business

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:57.827
was to recruit a
V.P. of Engineering.

00:03:57.827 --> 00:04:00.410
I talked to several people and
I found the guy I really liked,

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:01.670
his name was Shell.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:03.620
He was with the
company for six years

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:08.030
and was a huge instrumental
force in the company

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.340
and in the early days--

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:13.520
not single-handedly of
course, but in large part--

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.300
led all of the
software engineering

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.740
that was amazon.com.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:24.600
He had nothing to do with the
look of this website, though.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:30.210
This is what our website looked
like when we launched in 1995.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.105
I wrote all of
that HTML myself--

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:33.580
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:34.080
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.246
--please hold your applause.

00:04:35.246 --> 00:04:41.130
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.080
But Shell did the
hard part, he wrote

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.550
all of the code that
actually made it work.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:51.000
Before hiring Shell, I
was making our desks out

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.862
of doors and four by fours.

00:04:53.862 --> 00:04:55.320
It took me three
months to convince

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.380
Shell to come join this
company because it was really

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:59.550
just a one person company.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:03.010
It was me and it was a
piece of paper and a name.

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:06.290
And the name wasn't even Amazon
that time, it was Cadabra.

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:08.532
Cadabra got changed
because whenever

00:05:08.532 --> 00:05:10.240
I would say it to
anybody over the phone,

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:11.615
they would think
I said cadaver--

00:05:11.615 --> 00:05:12.930
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.900
--and I realized that wasn't
going to work very well.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.830
Amazon was incorporated,
by the way, by I--

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:21.210
I only knew two
people in Seattle

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.340
so we're driving out there,
I wanted to be moving fast.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.570
I wanted the company
be incorporated

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:25.540
by the time we arrived.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:28.170
So I called one of two
friends I had in Seattle

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.310
and said, could you
recommend a lawyer who

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:32.456
could help me get a
company Incorporated

00:05:32.456 --> 00:05:34.080
and open bank accounts
for the company.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.240
And do some stuff so I can
hit the ground running.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.100
My friend said, sure.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.220
So amazon.com was incorporated
by my friend's divorce lawyer--

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:42.680
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:46.560
--not like some big
high-powered attorney.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.650
But anyway, I finally talked
Shell into coming and joining

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:52.970
this intrepid venture.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:56.910
But before he had moved from
the Bay Area up to Seattle--

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.580
he was getting ready
to do that move.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.840
I called him and I said,
Shell, how tall do you

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.040
want your desk to be?

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:06.320
Because I'm making
them out of doors

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:09.200
I bought at Home Depot
and four by fours.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.240
There was like 10 seconds
of silence on the line

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.150
and I was thinking
to myself, oh great,

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.820
now he realizes what a two-bit
operation this really is

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:18.410
and he's going to
change his mind.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:20.586
He eventually
answered the question.

00:06:20.586 --> 00:06:21.960
About a year later
I said, Shell,

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.543
do you remember when I asked you
how tall you wanted your desk

00:06:24.543 --> 00:06:26.000
to be a year ago?

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.980
You were quiet for
like 10 seconds,

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:30.852
were you like reconsidering
your decision?

00:06:30.852 --> 00:06:32.810
And he said, no I was
just considering how tall

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:33.870
I wanted my desk to be.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:37.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:39.860
So we got the software put
together-- took about a year

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:42.530
to get the software put together
and to write this astonishingly

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:45.320
beautiful HTML.

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:46.760
It's really ugly, isn't it?

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:47.260
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:48.270
It is.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:49.340
And if you'll see--

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:51.890
I think the next
slide coming up-- no,

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:53.690
well this is our
next real office.

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:54.552
This is what--

00:06:54.552 --> 00:06:56.510
I don't have what our
website looks like today,

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:59.240
but you guys know what our
website looks like today.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:02.450
You will note, that it has
some minor improvements

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:07.910
like a search box
on the main page.

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:10.400
Here you had to actually
click through to search.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.090
You had to click on that link
that says, 1 million titles.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:15.680
So things have gotten better.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.634
By the way, since people don't
know this, our number one--

00:07:19.634 --> 00:07:21.800
the number one thing we've
invested in at amazon.com

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.390
has been technology.

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:30.020
And in fact, since 1997, we
have-- in the last five years--

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:33.110
we've spent $800
million on technology.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:36.670
So when you look at what it is
today and look back at this--

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:38.450
that's what $800
million will buy.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:45.020
We've only spent $300 million
on five million square feet

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:47.270
of fulfillment
centers, for example.

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:50.270
And only $600
million on marketing.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.565
So technology has been our
single biggest investment

00:07:52.565 --> 00:07:56.480
and the company basically
runs on computer science.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:58.510
It's either that or
it's just my bias

00:07:58.510 --> 00:07:59.750
since that's my background.

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:02.990
But at any rate,
that's what we do.

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:06.470
We really focus on trying to
differentiate our experience

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:13.010
with the engineering and the
algorithms that we put into it.

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:16.880
So anyway, we got this company
just about ready to launch

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:20.660
and we looked at it and looking
at this little tiny fulfillment

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:23.610
center-- we were here by
then, in this building.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:25.160
We had a basement
fulfillment center.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.590
To call it a fulfillment
center was very grand.

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:28.910
There's a lot of puffery.

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:32.870
It was 400 square feet, which
is about the size of a one car

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:34.610
garage.

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.730
We launched this business
and we're looking at it

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:39.830
and we didn't know if
anybody would order from us.

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:40.980
We really didn't.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:43.684
And in fact, one of
the software engineers

00:08:43.684 --> 00:08:45.350
looked at this little
space and he said,

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:48.740
I can't figure out whether
this is incredibly optimistic

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:49.970
or hopelessly pathetic.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:51.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:52.551
Indeed, we didn't know.

00:08:52.551 --> 00:08:54.050
We had no idea
whether anybody would

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:55.341
want to buy things in this way.

00:08:55.341 --> 00:08:58.850
The business plan called
for generating sales

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:01.592
very slowly as customers
change their attitudes.

00:09:01.592 --> 00:09:03.800
The original business plan--
which I thought was very

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.420
optimistic at the time--

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:08.690
called for amazon.com
to generate $70 million

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:11.210
in sales in the year 2001.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:13.880
We actually generated
in excess of $3 billion

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:16.234
in sales in the year 2001.

00:09:16.234 --> 00:09:17.900
We knew, by the way,
that we were really

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:20.030
on to something in
those first 30 days.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:24.290
In the first 30
days we got orders

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:27.230
from all 50 states and
45 different countries.

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:28.852
With not a dollar
of advertising,

00:09:28.852 --> 00:09:29.810
just all word of mouth.

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:32.480
In the first year we didn't
spend anything on advertising.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:34.010
All of it was word of mouth.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:35.964
And that really
forged the company.

00:09:35.964 --> 00:09:37.880
We were going to focus
on customer experience.

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:40.500
Because we saw the power
of word of mouth so very,

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.200
very clearly in
those early days.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:44.580
Something great about
word of mouth online,

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:46.710
by the way-- and
feedback from customers--

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:49.370
which is that email turns
off the politeness gene

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:50.420
in the human being.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:51.140
It's wonderful.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:54.560
So people actually tell
you what they really think.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:56.090
Usually in all caps.

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:59.570
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:03.650
So we get lots of info and
feedback from our customers

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:07.260
and we try to use it well.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:10.555
Well, in that first
few months, we

00:10:10.555 --> 00:10:12.930
got one order-- it was literally
in the first six months,

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:17.210
I remember exactly when it
was-- an order from Bulgaria.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:19.730
I didn't even know they
had internet access in 1995

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:22.310
in Bulgaria, but they did.

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:25.640
This person did not
pay with a credit card.

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:28.020
They paid with cash.

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.590
Which is a method of
payment we discourage.

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:34.790
They had taken two
crisp $100 bills

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:37.580
and folded them up in
a tiny little package.

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:39.500
And they took those
$100 bills and put them

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:41.330
inside a floppy disk.

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:43.100
They opened the
little metal door

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:45.320
and slipped them
in the floppy disk.

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:49.130
And then they mailed
us the floppy disk.

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:52.310
And on the note-- on the
floppy disk was a little note

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:57.490
and it said, the money is
inside the floppy disk.

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.510
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:05.170
And then it went on to
say, the customs inspectors

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:07.570
steal the money, but
they don't read English.

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:13.510
So we opened up the
little door sure enough,

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:14.590
there was $200 in there.

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:16.490
They had written their
order number on it

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:18.690
so we could associate it with--

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.940
and we shipped them their
books and all was well.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:30.051
Our vision has
changed a little bit

00:11:30.051 --> 00:11:31.800
but it hasn't changed
in about four years.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:34.210
About four years ago, we
came up with the notion

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:36.160
that we wanted to be a place--

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:37.840
build a place where
people can come

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:40.030
to find and discover
anything that they

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:41.800
might want to buy online.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:44.710
Literally universal selection.

00:11:44.710 --> 00:11:46.570
Anything with a capital "A."

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:49.240
We had already been on the path
of trying to be what we call,

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:51.019
Earth's most customer
centric company.

00:11:51.019 --> 00:11:52.060
And we mean three things.

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:53.434
We have a very
precise definition

00:11:53.434 --> 00:11:56.520
for what customer
centricity is at amazon.com.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:12:01.180
It means listen,
invent, and personalize.

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:03.790
The basic idea is
you can't really

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:06.651
run a business of any kind, I
think in most people's opinion,

00:12:06.651 --> 00:12:08.650
if you're not going to
listen to your customers.

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:09.920
That sort of fundamental.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:12.700
But invention is
equally important.

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:15.430
And invention, it goes far
beyond listening to customers.

00:12:15.430 --> 00:12:17.260
Because oftentimes,
customers don't really

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:19.130
know what it is that they want.

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:20.854
It's your job to
invent on their behalf

00:12:20.854 --> 00:12:22.270
and we've done a
tremendous amount

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:26.230
of invention and innovation of
different kinds in the company.

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:27.980
I'll tell you a little
bit more about that

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:30.940
when I give you the live demo.

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:32.560
And then finally
personalization.

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:34.870
The notion of
customer centricity

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:39.580
is to build a place where
each individual customer has

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:41.220
his or her own website.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:44.920
And to do that in large part,
not by explicit information

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:48.880
that the customer provides, but
through implicit observation

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:51.010
of their behavior
on the website.

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:54.280
So that we personalize that
experience based on the actions

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:55.380
that they take.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:57.310
And we put a lot of
effort into that.

00:12:57.310 --> 00:12:58.960
We've been working on that for--

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:00.820
well for all but
for the first year

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:02.545
of the company's operations.

00:13:02.545 --> 00:13:06.280
So for, I guess
about six years now.

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:09.700
A lot of-- some of our smartest
people have worked on that.

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:14.110
There are bunch of areas
where good algorithms,

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:16.450
good heuristics,
and so on, turn out

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:19.370
to be very important for
driving our customer experience.

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:21.580
And that kind of discovery--

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:23.720
accelerating discovery
for customers,

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:27.190
which is what personalization
is largely all about,

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:30.310
is critical when you use this.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:33.010
When you decide to be
selection intensive.

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:37.030
So we have over 28 million,
or something like that,

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:39.280
items in our catalog.

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:41.230
We have-- across all categories.

00:13:43.859 --> 00:13:45.400
When you have so
many items, you have

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:49.000
to work hard to build tools that
help customers find products.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:51.310
But you also have to
work hard at something

00:13:51.310 --> 00:13:54.490
which is a little less intuitive
and actually, technically,

00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:56.330
even more challenging.

00:13:56.330 --> 00:13:59.230
Which is to help
products find customers.

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:01.040
So we reverse the sense of that.

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:04.370
We've put a lot of energy into
helping products find customers

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:07.080
and it's paid off.

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:09.490
This is the basic thing
that we work on every day.

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:12.630
So what we want to do is have
the world's best customer

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:13.840
experience.

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:16.050
And there are a few things
that feed into that.

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:18.840
Massive selection-- which
requires great discovery

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:19.830
tools--

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:20.910
and low prices.

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:23.400
Those are the two
biggest things.

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:25.260
Low prices-- and both
of those things--

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:27.390
the heart of both
things is technology.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:30.260
It's very interesting
because I often get asked--

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:34.380
and again maybe it's just my
bias because I'm an EECS geek--

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:39.530
but how our business differs
from traditional retail.

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:41.910
The answer to that question--

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:46.470
I happen to know the guy who
does real estate for Starbucks.

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:49.170
And believe me,
this is like the guy

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:51.180
who has the metaphorical
corner office.

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:53.560
I mean, that business is
all about real estate.

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:55.650
This is why the old retail saw--

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.780
the three most important
things in retail

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:01.180
are location,
location, location.

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:02.720
And in our business
it's technology,

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:04.030
technology, technology.

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:06.360
And one of the great
things about technology

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:10.680
as opposed to real estate, is
that real estate on average--

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:12.000
economists will tell you--

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.920
will get more expensive
at the rate of inflation.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:17.450
So real estate gets more
expensive every year.

00:15:17.450 --> 00:15:20.880
And that's the core ingredient
of physical retailing.

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:25.350
Whereas our core ingredients
get to follow Moore's law.

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:28.320
So disk space getting twice
as cheap every 12 months.

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:30.990
Bandwidth getting twice as
cheap about at that rate.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:32.850
CPU every 18 months.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.650
This is a tremendous advantage.

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:37.200
As those things get cheaper
and cheaper and cheaper--

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:40.200
in and of themselves, they
don't offer any real benefit

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:41.500
to customers.

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:45.270
But what we get to do is
take all that compute power

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:48.030
and layer innovation
on top of it

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:51.910
to really change the
customer experience game.

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:54.740
And that's a lot of fun.

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.550
We have invested
enough in our software

00:15:57.550 --> 00:15:59.150
that we've become the more--

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:01.775
a big part of what we've done is
become more classic e-commerce

00:16:01.775 --> 00:16:02.340
company.

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:04.770
A more classic
technology company, too.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:07.660
Where we actually now service--

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:12.070
make available as an
application service provider--

00:16:12.070 --> 00:16:15.510
is an ASP model, our
e-commerce technology--

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:16.960
to others.

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:18.400
We also have done--

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:19.900
and I think Rob is
going to give you

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:22.110
guys a quick demo of our web
services stuff in a minute,

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:22.609
too.

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:26.575
Are we still doing
that competition?

00:16:26.575 --> 00:16:27.200
FREDERICK: Yes.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:30.010
BEZOS: Oh, good.

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:32.617
This is what-- instead of our
400 square foot fulfillment

00:16:32.617 --> 00:16:34.450
center, we now have
five million square feet

00:16:34.450 --> 00:16:36.940
of full of fulfillment space.

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:38.920
It turns out that
operations research

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:42.244
and all the logistic stuff we do
here is astonishingly similar.

00:16:42.244 --> 00:16:44.660
When I started learning about
these techniques I was like,

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:47.110
wait a second this
is computer science,

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:49.340
they just have different
words for everything.

00:16:51.972 --> 00:16:53.680
One of the things
that's very interesting

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:56.740
is, in some of these
fulfillment centers,

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:59.410
we have two million
different items

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:00.910
in the fulfillment center.

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:04.150
And you can order
any two of those

00:17:04.150 --> 00:17:07.660
and somehow, we have to marry
those two items together

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:10.839
and get them into a single
box and ship them to you.

00:17:10.839 --> 00:17:13.061
That sortation is
very challenging

00:17:13.061 --> 00:17:15.310
and there are many pieces
of it that are challenging--

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:16.930
to do in an efficient way.

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:18.730
All of the most
obvious things you

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:21.220
would try first turn
out-- they work but they

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:22.190
don't work efficiently.

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:24.650
The cost would be too high.

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:28.660
And what you end up doing
is, using very sophisticated

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:31.179
algorithms to calculate
an optimal pick path

00:17:31.179 --> 00:17:32.470
through the fulfillment center.

00:17:32.470 --> 00:17:35.950
you pick a bunch of
customer orders at once

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:38.080
and a decentralized
swarm of people

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:39.855
actually pick all these things.

00:17:39.855 --> 00:17:41.230
And then they have
to get married

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:43.480
and it's sort of a
two stage sort later.

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:47.440
But the picking
algorithms are like

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:49.600
a dynamic traveling
salesman problem

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:52.500
where the cities occasionally
move and disappear.

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:53.500
It's like unbelievably--

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:56.675
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:00.580
--it's actually very fun.

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:01.840
And I didn't know--

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:03.442
when I started the
business, I thought

00:18:03.442 --> 00:18:04.900
the front end of
the business would

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:06.700
be very fun and
intellectually stimulating

00:18:06.700 --> 00:18:09.880
and had envisioned a lot of
the kind of personalization

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:11.797
features that we
could build online.

00:18:11.797 --> 00:18:14.380
But one of the things that I had
not known and not envisioned,

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:17.560
is how intellectually
stimulating inventory

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:19.374
optimization and pick
path optimization

00:18:19.374 --> 00:18:21.040
and these kinds of
things would be, too.

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:24.220
This is kind of like
airline routing and so on.

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:25.990
It's a very interesting problem.

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:28.900
AUDIENCE: You wrote your
own software for that?

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:31.010
BEZOS: We wrote our
own software for that.

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:32.800
So it's a great question.

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:36.770
In fact, we bought off-the-shelf
inventory optimization

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:37.270
software.

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:38.860
There is no good
picking software

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:40.970
for the two million
items so that we

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:43.300
built from the very beginning.

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:45.430
We did buy inventory
optimization software

00:18:45.430 --> 00:18:49.180
and it didn't work very well
for such a large SKU base.

00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:50.710
One of the things
that we found, is

00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:52.270
that our operations
are significantly

00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:55.240
different from most companies
for a variety of reasons.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:57.550
Your typical mail order
company-- most of these things

00:18:57.550 --> 00:18:59.900
are made for like
warehouse management system

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:01.644
and made for mail
order companies.

00:19:01.644 --> 00:19:03.310
And you can buy the
things off the shelf

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:07.030
but they typically
have a different number

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:09.640
of SKUs measured in
the tens of thousands.

00:19:09.640 --> 00:19:13.000
And a lot of things change
when you scale that up

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:17.230
to millions of SKUs instead
of tens of thousands of SKUs.

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:18.980
And so we had to do a
lot of it ourselves.

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:22.900
We bought-- early on we
bought some personalization

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:25.124
software that also didn't work.

00:19:25.124 --> 00:19:27.040
And one of the things
that's interesting about

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:28.259
personalization--

00:19:28.259 --> 00:19:29.800
and that's where we
put a huge amount

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:31.750
of energy and attention--

00:19:31.750 --> 00:19:36.340
is that first of all,
it has to work at scale

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:38.410
with millions of customers.

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:42.770
And on peak days we
will take orders for

00:19:42.770 --> 00:19:44.920
and ship 2 million items.

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:50.880
And so it's very, very
sensitive to doing that--

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:53.650
and we do for
unrecognized customers,

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:57.730
we do real-time personalization
based on their click stream.

00:19:57.730 --> 00:19:59.600
So there really is
nobody doing that.

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:04.490
The other thing is, that we
can do active experiments.

00:20:04.490 --> 00:20:07.360
This is like a really big
point that most people miss.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:11.050
But it's one thing to
take a huge data set

00:20:11.050 --> 00:20:14.770
and sort of do backward
looking data mining on it.

00:20:14.770 --> 00:20:18.670
And you can actually find some
interesting things that way.

00:20:18.670 --> 00:20:20.920
But what you can't
get from that,

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:22.510
is anything that
has a feedback loop.

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:24.820
Where if you change
something, changing that thing

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:28.510
is actually going to change
the customer behavior.

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:34.000
So one of the most fascinating
tools we have at our disposal,

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:37.720
is the ability to do
active experiments.

00:20:37.720 --> 00:20:42.160
It's kind of this huge
laboratory with literally,

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:45.550
30 million visitors a
month coming through.

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:48.610
And every time we make a
little change on the website,

00:20:48.610 --> 00:20:51.190
we roll that change out
to a randomly selected

00:20:51.190 --> 00:20:53.890
group of people and
hold constant the rest.

00:20:53.890 --> 00:20:55.870
So we call it A/B testing.

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:58.185
We may do-- half the
people see version A

00:20:58.185 --> 00:21:00.560
and half see version B. They're
seeing it simultaneously,

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:02.629
so we're holding all
the factors constant.

00:21:02.629 --> 00:21:03.670
And we can actually tell.

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:05.680
So if we develop a new
personalization algorithm

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:08.790
that some smart person inside
the company comes up with,

00:21:08.790 --> 00:21:11.200
and they're really jazzed
and they think it's better,

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:13.730
we never have to
argue about that.

00:21:13.730 --> 00:21:15.520
We say, well let's try it.

00:21:15.520 --> 00:21:19.030
And a few hours later, we
will know whether it's better.

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:22.330
And that is huge because
those kinds of arguments

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:26.320
are so useless and
energy draining.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:27.490
Come on up.

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:28.880
I'm going to introduce--

00:21:28.880 --> 00:21:31.030
this is Robert.

00:21:31.030 --> 00:21:31.690
You're an alum.

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:32.560
FREDERICK: Yes I am.

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:34.420
BEZOS: And Robert has been
with us for quite a while.

00:21:34.420 --> 00:21:35.526
How long have you been?

00:21:35.526 --> 00:21:37.310
FREDERICK: About
3 and 1/2 years.

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:38.560
BEZOS: 3 and 1/2 years.

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:40.180
We've been to Japan together.

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:43.360
We've been through
several trials together.

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:44.790
All good things.

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:46.900
You've worked on our
wireless business

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:49.040
and now you're doing a
lot with web services.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:51.230
Let me turn it over to you.

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:53.340
FREDERICK: So again, my
name is Robert Frederick.

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:56.430
I manage amazon.com's
web services program.

00:21:56.430 --> 00:22:00.500
How many of you have
heard of web services?

00:22:00.500 --> 00:22:03.320
Or are doing things
with web services?

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:05.540
Okay, great.

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:09.470
Amazon Web Services is something
that we launched this year

00:22:09.470 --> 00:22:11.030
in July.

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:15.730
We found that by
providing an API--

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:17.720
a solid SDK or an API--

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:23.570
to our broad website developers
as well as our associates,

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:27.830
that we would be able to get
more and more users to interact

00:22:27.830 --> 00:22:30.980
with our features
all over the internet

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:33.110
rather than just
Amazon's website.

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:38.180
So what we were thinking of
doing is, we had three goals.

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:42.140
We wanted to define a product.

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:46.700
And that product is the ability
to create your own store

00:22:46.700 --> 00:22:48.740
on your own website.

00:22:48.740 --> 00:22:53.300
To expose Amazon
services and features on,

00:22:53.300 --> 00:22:54.770
I believe it at
this point in time,

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:57.860
it's 900,000 websites that
interact with this on a day

00:22:57.860 --> 00:23:00.270
by day basis.

00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:04.250
So what we were doing is, we
were defining a capability

00:23:04.250 --> 00:23:05.030
to interact.

00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:06.470
To get search results.

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:09.740
To interact with our
similarities engine.

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:14.150
To find products and to
build those storefronts

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:17.570
on external websites
all over the internet

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:19.490
in various countries.

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:23.930
All through exposing
a solid API and SDK.

00:23:23.930 --> 00:23:28.610
So again, we had a market
of 900,000 websites.

00:23:28.610 --> 00:23:34.580
We have developers who wanted
to build SOAP packages, Scripts,

00:23:34.580 --> 00:23:39.440
Perl, PHP, you name
it-- .net applications.

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:41.420
And we wanted to provide
those applications

00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:43.970
to each one of these
web site owners

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:48.410
so that they can use our tools
and interact with us via soap

00:23:48.410 --> 00:23:55.010
or via XML over HTTP, in a
very easy and viable solution.

00:23:55.010 --> 00:23:57.380
So the economic model
for this as well--

00:23:57.380 --> 00:23:58.770
or why are we doing this?

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:01.610
Why would Amazon ever want
to expose the services

00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:05.870
that we have on our site to
all of these other websites

00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:07.140
on the internet?

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:08.700
And the reason is very simple.

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:12.200
The more people that are
interacting with our products,

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:14.900
the more items that
will potentially sell.

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:16.850
And then we also
have the capability

00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:19.340
to provide these
products on their website

00:24:19.340 --> 00:24:24.620
in a way that doesn't have to
make them recreate the wheel.

00:24:24.620 --> 00:24:26.300
It's easy to implement.

00:24:26.300 --> 00:24:31.790
Literally, anywhere between five
minutes to a couple of hours,

00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:35.090
and people are able to have
a very robust storefront.

00:24:35.090 --> 00:24:38.880
A very robust way of
interacting with our products

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:40.091
and our selection.

00:24:43.250 --> 00:24:44.990
What we ended up
doing was, we came up

00:24:44.990 --> 00:24:49.304
with a SOAP interface and
a XML over HTTP interface.

00:24:49.304 --> 00:24:51.470
And I just wanted to go
through a couple of examples

00:24:51.470 --> 00:24:52.844
of some of the
things that people

00:24:52.844 --> 00:24:56.590
have built before turning
this over to Jeff again.

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:07.870
So here is a very simple script.

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:10.780
It's called Amazon light--

00:25:10.780 --> 00:25:12.010
simple search.

00:25:12.010 --> 00:25:15.190
What we did here was, we
provided the capability

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:19.090
for this one developer
to put this little search

00:25:19.090 --> 00:25:21.730
box over here on his site.

00:25:21.730 --> 00:25:23.260
And if I do a search for dogs--

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:29.010
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:29.010 --> 00:25:30.000
--sorry.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:33.810
So as you'll notice
over here on the right--

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:35.580
and this is kind of cool.

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:40.120
Over here on the right,
when you type in dogs,

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:44.700
you will be able to interact
with us in a very quick manner.

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:48.570
Send a request over to
us via XML over HTTP,

00:25:48.570 --> 00:25:51.510
or via SOAP-- it all depends
on the back end system.

00:25:51.510 --> 00:25:54.900
That request is then parsed
by our back end services.

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:56.970
It retrieves information
from our back end

00:25:56.970 --> 00:25:59.940
systems, our databases
and our search engine.

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:05.010
And then sends back an XML file
to this particular website's

00:26:05.010 --> 00:26:05.940
script.

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:08.220
They then use that
information and embed it

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:10.080
into that existing site.

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:13.920
The very cool thing down
here, is it says quick start.

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:19.580
And this is where people will be
able to configure their website

00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:20.340
offering.

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:23.670
Down here, by answering a
couple of these search questions

00:26:23.670 --> 00:26:26.460
or these questions right here.

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:29.670
And then they'll be able to
copy the code and just embedded

00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:32.520
into their site and
it auto-magically--

00:26:32.520 --> 00:26:34.110
it now allows them to--

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:34.890
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:38.130
--do search on their
own site without having

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:39.880
to actually build the
whole search engine

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:41.310
and the whole technology.

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:45.810
You click on that link up here
for these top search results

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:48.420
and you're brought
over to an Amazon site

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:52.380
or you're brought over to
a Amazon light site that

00:26:52.380 --> 00:26:54.900
is powered by that third party.

00:26:54.900 --> 00:26:58.460
Let me go ahead and click
on one of the links.

00:26:58.460 --> 00:27:00.340
So this will bring
you over to Amazon.

00:27:00.340 --> 00:27:02.740
Now the reason--
or why in the world

00:27:02.740 --> 00:27:04.840
would anyone want to embed
this on their website?

00:27:04.840 --> 00:27:08.110
Well, we've tied the
web services offering

00:27:08.110 --> 00:27:10.630
to our Associates
Program, which would

00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:12.940
allow the developer
or the website owner

00:27:12.940 --> 00:27:16.870
to earn a percentage for
every item sold on their site.

00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:20.230
So every time they're bringing
a customer over to Amazon,

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:22.750
they actually are
given the opportunity

00:27:22.750 --> 00:27:24.190
to earn a referral fee.

00:27:24.190 --> 00:27:28.940
Anywhere between 5% and 15% of
the actual price of the item.

00:27:28.940 --> 00:27:30.570
So there's a built in model--

00:27:30.570 --> 00:27:34.955
a built in reason why people
would want to use this.

00:27:34.955 --> 00:27:36.580
I know I only have
a little bit of time

00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:40.070
so I'm going to go really fast.

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:48.140
So there's also a very
interesting implementation

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:51.416
called Book Search
or Book Watch--

00:27:51.416 --> 00:27:54.090
as soon as it opens up.

00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:56.420
What this site will do,
when it actually changes,

00:27:56.420 --> 00:28:01.220
is it's going to make a call
to Google's web services

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:05.140
offering and Amazon's
web services offering,

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:08.010
as well as this other web
service offering called

00:28:08.010 --> 00:28:09.730
Book Watch Plus.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:11.700
And it'll go-- or
On Focus, sorry--

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:15.900
and it'll go through
and find various topics

00:28:15.900 --> 00:28:19.560
all on the internet via
various peoples web logs.

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:22.560
Then it will call Google
and then determine

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:25.150
what the subjects
are for these people

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:27.360
or this particular topic.

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:29.130
And then it'll call
Amazon and find

00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:31.350
products related to those
books that people are

00:28:31.350 --> 00:28:33.390
talking about in their weblogs.

00:28:33.390 --> 00:28:35.880
So what you have here,
is an opportunity

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:40.260
to see the item that people are
talking about in their weblogs.

00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:44.220
To see the news items that
are all over the internet

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:46.440
concerning that particular item.

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:49.260
And then to see Amazon's
similarity engine being

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:52.320
used to provide products
that people are also

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:55.170
buying on our web site.

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:58.380
So this is an amalgamation--
or a combination--

00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:03.480
of three different web
services displaying a page so

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:07.290
that a customer would have a
different user interface that

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:09.800
would allow them to find
out more information

00:29:09.800 --> 00:29:12.540
or to have a different
customer experience.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:14.790
A better customer
experience for the things

00:29:14.790 --> 00:29:16.110
that they're interested in.

00:29:16.110 --> 00:29:19.140
This is the "surprise
us" type of thing.

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:21.660
This is what we were very
interested in seeing-- people

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:25.350
coming up with new ways of
consuming our web services

00:29:25.350 --> 00:29:27.810
offerings to do things
that we had not even

00:29:27.810 --> 00:29:28.830
thought of ourselves.

