Buy together, it's only $19.96.

00:38:41.930 --> 00:38:44.400
All of that product clustering
is done automatically,

00:38:44.400 --> 00:38:46.550
of course.

00:38:46.550 --> 00:38:49.160
Come down and you'll
notice that customers who

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:50.810
bought Slave Girls--

00:38:50.810 --> 00:38:52.670
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:52.670 --> 00:38:58.910
--also bought Sorority Babes
and The Slime Ball Bowl-O-Rama

00:38:58.910 --> 00:39:01.280
and my personal
favorite, Cannibal Women

00:39:01.280 --> 00:39:03.000
in the Avocado Jungle of Death.

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:05.220
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:05.220 --> 00:39:07.560
And there's this
interesting thing here.

00:39:07.560 --> 00:39:10.320
We even know when things
are part of a series.

00:39:10.320 --> 00:39:13.170
And also they bought DVDs from
the Barbarian Queen series.

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:16.190
So we can click through and
look at the Barbarian Queen

00:39:16.190 --> 00:39:20.352
and there's a
customer review here.

00:39:20.352 --> 00:39:23.150
It says, "For Lana
Clarkson fans only.

00:39:23.150 --> 00:39:24.890
This is Barbarian Queen 2.

00:39:24.890 --> 00:39:26.780
This is not even
Barbarian Queen.

00:39:26.780 --> 00:39:31.510
It's an incredibly bad film
whose only redeeming feature

00:39:31.510 --> 00:39:35.330
is Lana Clarkson in and
out of some skimpy outfits.

00:39:35.330 --> 00:39:36.800
She's completely
gorgeous but even

00:39:36.800 --> 00:39:39.260
she can't save a
disaster of a film."

00:39:39.260 --> 00:39:41.210
Then here comes the
great recommendation,

00:39:41.210 --> 00:39:42.650
"buy Death Stalker instead."

00:39:42.650 --> 00:39:45.824
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:51.923 --> 00:39:54.006
AUDIENCE: Everything you
had in the previous page,

00:39:54.006 --> 00:39:55.850
is that based on your inventory?

00:39:55.850 --> 00:39:57.990
Like what's next to each
other in your warehouse?

00:39:57.990 --> 00:39:58.489
BEZOS: No.

00:39:58.489 --> 00:40:00.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:00.380 --> 00:40:03.610
In fact-- were you
trying to be funny?

00:40:03.610 --> 00:40:04.110
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:04.110 --> 00:40:06.970
No, I didn't think so.

00:40:06.970 --> 00:40:10.420
No, it's not it's
very interesting.

00:40:10.420 --> 00:40:13.090
We use a technique
called Random Stow.

00:40:13.090 --> 00:40:16.700
So all of the items in the
warehouse are stowed randomly.

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:17.902
There is no rhyme or reason.

00:40:17.902 --> 00:40:18.610
AUDIENCE: Hashed.

00:40:18.610 --> 00:40:20.151
BEZOS: As you--
yeah, they're hashed.

00:40:20.151 --> 00:40:22.455
So as you walk through,
they all have locations.

00:40:22.455 --> 00:40:24.580
They all have BINs so you
know where everything is.

00:40:24.580 --> 00:40:26.830
But only the computer
knows where everything is.

00:40:26.830 --> 00:40:30.490
As you walk through,
it'll be Slave Girls

00:40:30.490 --> 00:40:34.690
From Beyond Infinity,
right next to Dr. Seuss.

00:40:34.690 --> 00:40:35.950
They'll be side by side.

00:40:35.950 --> 00:40:37.300
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:37.300 --> 00:40:40.180
And so it does make--
walking through,

00:40:40.180 --> 00:40:44.230
it is like an incredibly
eclectic experience,

00:40:44.230 --> 00:40:46.330
when you go walk through
the fulfillment center.

00:40:46.330 --> 00:40:49.720
But these similarities
are incredibly

00:40:49.720 --> 00:40:52.146
hard to compute well.

00:40:52.146 --> 00:40:54.520
And there's a lot of stuff--
you have to look at how much

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:56.140
information theory
there is in each--

00:40:56.140 --> 00:40:58.389
I mean how much information
there is in each purchase.

00:40:58.389 --> 00:41:01.420
Because you don't
want, you know--

00:41:01.420 --> 00:41:03.790
if you do this simple,
straightforward thing then

00:41:03.790 --> 00:41:07.000
every book just recommends,
oh and customers who bought

00:41:07.000 --> 00:41:09.460
this also bought Harry Potter.

00:41:09.460 --> 00:41:11.157
And that's not very interesting.

00:41:11.157 --> 00:41:12.740
So you have to take
that into account.

00:41:12.740 --> 00:41:14.352
You have to do a lot of things.

00:41:14.352 --> 00:41:16.560
And we have to compute
millions and millions of these

00:41:16.560 --> 00:41:18.400
and we have to do
it very rapidly.

00:41:18.400 --> 00:41:20.620
And we like to do
it on small machines

00:41:20.620 --> 00:41:24.040
so we figure out how to do that
in only two gigabytes of RAM.

00:41:24.040 --> 00:41:26.200
So there's a lot of--

00:41:26.200 --> 00:41:28.870
there's a lot of cool
stuff along that.

00:41:28.870 --> 00:41:31.720
There was a great similarity,
I remember one time,

00:41:31.720 --> 00:41:35.149
I was looking at this book
and it was a book on zen.

00:41:35.149 --> 00:41:37.690
And I looked down at the list
of similarities-- customers who

00:41:37.690 --> 00:41:39.648
bought this book on zen
also bought-- and there

00:41:39.648 --> 00:41:42.370
were five or six similarities
as they usually are.

00:41:42.370 --> 00:41:45.760
And five of the six were
other books about zen

00:41:45.760 --> 00:41:50.110
and then the sixth one was How
to Have a Clutter Free Desk.

00:41:50.110 --> 00:41:51.510
And that's the
kind of connection

00:41:51.510 --> 00:41:53.170
that only a computer can make.

00:41:53.170 --> 00:41:55.810
I mean no human editor is going
to say-- they would always

00:41:55.810 --> 00:41:58.630
pick six other books on zen.

00:41:58.630 --> 00:42:01.180
But you know, people who
like zen also buy books

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:05.820
on how to have a clutter
free desk and that comes out.

00:42:05.820 --> 00:42:07.430
Let's see.

00:42:07.430 --> 00:42:13.160
I'll show you one of
my favorite features.

00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:14.610
I can show it just
by any product.

00:42:14.610 --> 00:42:17.546
I've bought so many
things on the site.

00:42:17.546 --> 00:42:24.440
But I'm going to show
you the OXO Salad

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:28.190
Spinner in our kitchen store.

00:42:28.190 --> 00:42:28.690
Here it is.

00:42:28.690 --> 00:42:31.400
The OXO Good Grips
Salad Spinner.

00:42:31.400 --> 00:42:35.050
Now, this is interesting
for a couple of reasons.

00:42:35.050 --> 00:42:39.070
One is that the customer
reviews on this product read

00:42:39.070 --> 00:42:40.430
like a sexual experience.

00:42:40.430 --> 00:42:40.930
I mean--

00:42:40.930 --> 00:42:41.480
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:41.480 --> 00:42:45.310
--people love the OXO.

00:42:45.310 --> 00:42:48.850
People who don't eat salad
love the OXO Salad Spinner.

00:42:48.850 --> 00:42:53.200
That's how potent of
a product this is.

00:42:53.200 --> 00:42:57.260
And we do a couple of things
here that are interesting.

00:42:57.260 --> 00:42:59.500
One is, you can
see here it says,

00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:02.410
these friends have
purchased these items.

00:43:02.410 --> 00:43:05.620
These are friends of mine.

00:43:05.620 --> 00:43:09.190
And so as long as we're here,
and as long as I've already

00:43:09.190 --> 00:43:11.129
identified my friends,
we might as well--

00:43:11.129 --> 00:43:12.670
whenever I stumble
across something I

00:43:12.670 --> 00:43:15.810
get to see which of my friends
have bought this thing.

00:43:15.810 --> 00:43:19.690
By the way, doing that in
real time isn't that easy.

00:43:19.690 --> 00:43:21.640
Remember this is a big
decentralized system

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:25.450
with thousands of web servers
and you don't want to go back.

00:43:25.450 --> 00:43:29.214
What happens is, we use
a pub/sub technology.

00:43:29.214 --> 00:43:31.630
And when you first come to our
website, the instant you're

00:43:31.630 --> 00:43:35.440
recognized, we publish
a message to all

00:43:35.440 --> 00:43:38.290
of the different
services that subscribe.

00:43:38.290 --> 00:43:41.560
The ones that subscribe to that
message recognize it and they

00:43:41.560 --> 00:43:44.050
start immediately-- the
second you hit our gateway--

00:43:44.050 --> 00:43:47.170
they start filling a high
speed cache of everything

00:43:47.170 --> 00:43:49.060
that might be interesting.

00:43:49.060 --> 00:43:51.640
That you might want to see
on this visit to the store.

00:43:51.640 --> 00:43:53.140
So that's one of
the ways that we're

00:43:53.140 --> 00:43:55.540
able to do millions
and millions of visits.

00:43:55.540 --> 00:43:57.370
Provide this level
of personalization

00:43:57.370 --> 00:44:01.240
and customization and have it
not be really, really slow.

00:44:01.240 --> 00:44:04.540
The other thing that was very
personalized on this page.

00:44:04.540 --> 00:44:07.690
And this is-- I told you earlier
that we very carefully test

00:44:07.690 --> 00:44:09.580
all of our features.

00:44:09.580 --> 00:44:12.280
When we do this testing, we
have a very simple objective

00:44:12.280 --> 00:44:12.880
function.

00:44:12.880 --> 00:44:14.546
It's a little more
complicated than this

00:44:14.546 --> 00:44:17.290
but basically our
objective function is,

00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:18.610
we prefer the thing--

00:44:18.610 --> 00:44:21.370
if we're testing feature
A against feature B,

00:44:21.370 --> 00:44:24.520
we like the one that
generates more sales.

00:44:24.520 --> 00:44:28.480
We're pretty simple
minded in that regard.

00:44:28.480 --> 00:44:30.370
But this, what I'm
about to show you,

00:44:30.370 --> 00:44:33.310
is one of the few features
we've ever left on the web site

00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:35.800
even though with
statistical significance,

00:44:35.800 --> 00:44:38.330
it slightly reduces sales.

00:44:38.330 --> 00:44:40.420
And it's called the
Instant Order Update.

00:44:40.420 --> 00:44:43.480
So you can see up
there, at the very top

00:44:43.480 --> 00:44:45.850
it says, "Jeff
Bezos, you purchased

00:44:45.850 --> 00:44:48.850
this item on May 16th, 2000."

00:44:48.850 --> 00:44:52.900
So more than two years ago, I
bought the OXO Salad Spinner.

00:44:52.900 --> 00:44:55.120
Now with salad spinners it
doesn't happen very much.

00:44:55.120 --> 00:44:58.090
But with music CDs and a
whole bunch of other products,

00:44:58.090 --> 00:45:00.790
people forget
they've bought them.

00:45:00.790 --> 00:45:02.410
And they buy them a second time.

00:45:02.410 --> 00:45:05.440
[LAUGHING]

00:45:05.440 --> 00:45:07.780
So they're like,
I don't remember--

00:45:07.780 --> 00:45:10.780
they buy that Madonna CD
and then they're like--

00:45:10.780 --> 00:45:12.820
they get it home or maybe
they never notice it.

00:45:12.820 --> 00:45:15.280
But they buy it
accidentally a second time.

00:45:15.280 --> 00:45:18.190
And all of us do unless
you're like incredibly

00:45:18.190 --> 00:45:21.670
organized or something,
it's hard to avoid this.

00:45:21.670 --> 00:45:23.980
So you know, we're busy.

00:45:23.980 --> 00:45:27.280
People buy books a second
time and so on so on.

00:45:27.280 --> 00:45:29.570
So anyway, we thought
about this and we thought,

00:45:29.570 --> 00:45:30.880
well what we can do--

00:45:30.880 --> 00:45:33.730
unlike any other store
anywhere in the world--

00:45:33.730 --> 00:45:35.870
is every time you
come to a page,

00:45:35.870 --> 00:45:39.200
if you've already bought
it we'll just tell you.

00:45:39.200 --> 00:45:41.740
Then you won't buy
it a second time.

00:45:41.740 --> 00:45:45.110
Even though this
slightly reduces sales,

00:45:45.110 --> 00:45:48.520
we get so much positive
feedback from this.

00:45:48.520 --> 00:45:51.550
We get so many email
messages to customer service

00:45:51.550 --> 00:45:54.820
where people said, I was
just about to buy that Enya

00:45:54.820 --> 00:45:56.090
CD a second time--

00:45:56.090 --> 00:45:57.580
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:57.580 --> 00:45:58.490
--and I didn't.

00:45:58.490 --> 00:46:00.929
Thank you.

00:46:00.929 --> 00:46:02.720
It's like they're
thanking us, because they

00:46:02.720 --> 00:46:03.790
didn't buy something.

00:46:03.790 --> 00:46:07.030
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:46:07.030 --> 00:46:07.670
What's that?

00:46:07.670 --> 00:46:08.840
AUDIENCE: Do you
ever return items?

00:46:08.840 --> 00:46:10.465
BEZOS: Well you know,
the amazing thing

00:46:10.465 --> 00:46:12.804
is most people don't
return things very much.

00:46:12.804 --> 00:46:15.220
You know what they will do,
because it's a bit of a hassle

00:46:15.220 --> 00:46:18.100
to return something, if they got
a second copy of that Enya CD,

00:46:18.100 --> 00:46:20.350
typically they would give
it to a friend or something.

00:46:20.350 --> 00:46:22.016
They don't really
want it but it's not--

00:46:22.016 --> 00:46:24.385
for an expensive item, yes
people would return it.

00:46:24.385 --> 00:46:26.260
But people also tend to
keep track of their--

00:46:26.260 --> 00:46:28.147
they're not going to,
oh I already bought

00:46:28.147 --> 00:46:28.855
a digital camera.

00:46:28.855 --> 00:46:29.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:29.770 --> 00:46:33.440
Oh silly me.

00:46:33.440 --> 00:46:34.795
That's right I have a Segway!

00:46:34.795 --> 00:46:37.483
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:37.483 --> 00:46:37.982
Shoot!

00:46:37.982 --> 00:46:39.830
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:39.830 --> 00:46:43.120
But for a $12 CD, people
don't put the same amount

00:46:43.120 --> 00:46:46.150
of attention on it.

00:46:46.150 --> 00:46:52.636
So anyway, I'll just show you.

00:46:52.636 --> 00:46:53.760
This is just amazing to me.

00:46:53.760 --> 00:46:55.801
Every time I look at this,
there are new reviews.

00:46:55.801 --> 00:46:59.130
So this one was only
written on November 9th.

00:46:59.130 --> 00:47:02.370
And it's from this guy
named Warren Holzum,

00:47:02.370 --> 00:47:04.440
"this is one heck of a spinner.

00:47:04.440 --> 00:47:06.594
I've darn near gotten
up to centrifuge speeds

00:47:06.594 --> 00:47:07.760
with just a few easy pumps--

00:47:07.760 --> 00:47:09.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:09.870 --> 00:47:12.450
--great for drying off all
kinds of fruits and vegetables."

00:47:12.450 --> 00:47:13.740
I mean just goes on and on.

00:47:13.740 --> 00:47:15.980
Literally, there
are-- well let's see,

00:47:15.980 --> 00:47:17.434
it should say down here.

00:47:20.160 --> 00:47:23.610
I don't know how many
customers reviews there are.

00:47:23.610 --> 00:47:25.720
There are hundreds.

00:47:25.720 --> 00:47:28.560
Well here it only says 74.

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:30.060
I don't know why
there are only 74--

00:47:30.060 --> 00:47:31.143
I thought there were more.

00:47:31.143 --> 00:47:35.160
But anyway, there's 74
customer reviews here.

00:47:35.160 --> 00:47:38.040
Look this one is labeled
"Sand," from Georgina.

00:47:38.040 --> 00:47:39.990
"This is a very good
spinner, at a good price.

00:47:39.990 --> 00:47:41.698
Most of the previous
reviews are dead on.

00:47:41.698 --> 00:47:43.260
Except they neglect
to mention that

00:47:43.260 --> 00:47:46.080
this spinner does a great
job of removing sand

00:47:46.080 --> 00:47:48.390
from red lettuce and
other exotic greens.

00:47:48.390 --> 00:47:52.042
Including dandelion, radish,
not to mention spinach.

00:47:52.042 --> 00:47:53.500
I bought it for
that reason alone."

00:47:53.500 --> 00:47:56.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:56.070 --> 00:47:58.575
People are just
fanatical salad spinners.

00:48:01.812 --> 00:48:03.960
Look at this, it's
all five star reviews.

00:48:03.960 --> 00:48:07.350
Oh here's somebody, "good
but questionable durability."

00:48:07.350 --> 00:48:09.241
There's always one
in the crowd, here.

00:48:09.241 --> 00:48:13.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:13.380 --> 00:48:15.606
We can sort these reviews.

00:48:15.606 --> 00:48:17.480
It's not a totally
straightforward algorithm,

00:48:17.480 --> 00:48:21.810
but we can basically-- we
try to put the reviews--

00:48:21.810 --> 00:48:23.890
if you come back
to the main page--

00:48:23.890 --> 00:48:26.442
some of the top reviews
are from people who've

00:48:26.442 --> 00:48:27.650
gotten lots of helpful votes.

00:48:27.650 --> 00:48:30.220
So you can always vote-- was
this review helpful to you?

00:48:30.220 --> 00:48:31.630
Yes or no.

00:48:31.630 --> 00:48:33.740
One of the nice things
about voting on amazon.com,

00:48:33.740 --> 00:48:37.720
is we have customer identity
because everybody who's using

00:48:37.720 --> 00:48:41.350
our system has a credit card
number and so we can kind

00:48:41.350 --> 00:48:42.410
of keep track of--

00:48:42.410 --> 00:48:45.552
we don't let somebody stack
the deck and vote 100 times.

00:48:45.552 --> 00:48:48.010
I mean I guess it could if they
have a 100 different credit

00:48:48.010 --> 00:48:49.634
card numbers and 100
different accounts

00:48:49.634 --> 00:48:51.730
but it's not really
worth stacking

00:48:51.730 --> 00:48:55.176
the deck for the salad spinner
to get so many credit cards.

00:48:55.176 --> 00:48:56.960
AUDIENCE: Unless you make it.

00:48:56.960 --> 00:49:00.360
BEZOS: Well I guess that's true,
if you make it then why not?

00:49:00.360 --> 00:49:02.890
If you make it and you're evil.

00:49:02.890 --> 00:49:04.240
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:04.240 --> 00:49:06.340
We did have-- it was very funny.

00:49:06.340 --> 00:49:07.440
We do remove them.

00:49:07.440 --> 00:49:09.190
And I often think we
should at least build

00:49:09.190 --> 00:49:11.000
a graveyard for them somewhere.

00:49:11.000 --> 00:49:13.914
But we do have people who
put up spoof customer reviews

00:49:13.914 --> 00:49:14.830
and they're hilarious.

00:49:14.830 --> 00:49:16.332
We've had God review the Bible.

00:49:16.332 --> 00:49:19.710
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:23.370 --> 00:49:27.090
We had-- I can't
remember which Bronte

00:49:27.090 --> 00:49:31.050
sister it is, the one who
really hated Jane Austen.

00:49:31.050 --> 00:49:32.820
It was either
Emily or Charlotte.

00:49:32.820 --> 00:49:33.630
Does anybody know?

00:49:33.630 --> 00:49:34.296
AUDIENCE: Emily.

00:49:34.296 --> 00:49:35.910
BEZOS: Is it Emily?

00:49:35.910 --> 00:49:37.590
So we had Emily--

00:49:37.590 --> 00:49:41.850
a few years ago there
were like two mini series

00:49:41.850 --> 00:49:45.540
and a full length motion picture
based on different Jane Austen

00:49:45.540 --> 00:49:46.060
novels.

00:49:46.060 --> 00:49:47.430
This was just a few years ago.

00:49:47.430 --> 00:49:51.720
And somebody started posting
customer reviews pretending

00:49:51.720 --> 00:49:53.850
to be Emily Bronte.

00:49:53.850 --> 00:49:56.920
And she was just like,
I hate Jane Austen.

00:49:56.920 --> 00:50:02.100
Two mini series in a full length
motion picture in one year?

00:50:02.100 --> 00:50:06.810
They were-- it was quite
amusing but we take those down.

00:50:06.810 --> 00:50:10.560
We also had one guy who said--
his review was incredibly

00:50:10.560 --> 00:50:12.270
honest, it wasn't a spoof.

00:50:12.270 --> 00:50:15.270
But it was also kind of like
damning with faint praise.

00:50:15.270 --> 00:50:17.880
His review started out,
this is the best book

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:19.000
my brother ever wrote.

00:50:19.000 --> 00:50:22.213
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:23.590 --> 00:50:26.170
I'm like, golly
that's a tough family.

00:50:26.170 --> 00:50:28.000
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:28.000 --> 00:50:30.880
Unlike all my other
brothers books

00:50:30.880 --> 00:50:32.260
this one might be worth reading.

00:50:32.260 --> 00:50:34.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:34.900 --> 00:50:38.320
So let's see, I don't know
I might just switch over

00:50:38.320 --> 00:50:41.869
to Q&amp;A. You know,
once you go in I

00:50:41.869 --> 00:50:43.160
can show you the page you made.

00:50:43.160 --> 00:50:46.330
This is the real time
click stream stuff.

00:50:46.330 --> 00:50:50.500
It's more useful for customers
who we don't recognize.

00:50:50.500 --> 00:50:52.210
But it's you know,
trying to make

00:50:52.210 --> 00:50:54.990
sense of these bizarre
pages that we've looked at--

00:50:54.990 --> 00:50:59.680
The Dog Listener, Slave Girls,
and the OXO salad spinner.

00:50:59.680 --> 00:51:03.270
We're not giving it
much to work with here.

00:51:03.270 --> 00:51:07.577
But it's recommending the
Microplane Greater Zester.

00:51:07.577 --> 00:51:09.160
Which by the way, I
have that product.

00:51:09.160 --> 00:51:10.990
That's a great product.

00:51:10.990 --> 00:51:14.380
And it's recommending Assault
of the Killer Bimbos, the Dog

00:51:14.380 --> 00:51:17.470
Whisperer.

00:51:17.470 --> 00:51:20.860
It's also recommending the
mini salad and herb spinner.

00:51:20.860 --> 00:51:26.500
So if you need a herb spinner in
addition to your salad spinner.

00:51:26.500 --> 00:51:31.020
But I think I will
end the demo there.

00:51:31.020 --> 00:51:33.610
Let me see if I can get back.

00:51:33.610 --> 00:51:35.064
I don't even really need this.

00:51:35.064 --> 00:51:36.855
AUDIENCE: So these
recommendations, they're

00:51:36.855 --> 00:51:40.360
all based completely on
the customer experience?

00:51:40.360 --> 00:51:43.460
There's no variable of
how much money you make?

00:51:43.460 --> 00:51:44.960
BEZOS: The customer
recommendations

00:51:44.960 --> 00:51:48.650
are based on-- what we're trying
to do is get the probability.

00:51:48.650 --> 00:51:51.440
What we want is to increase
the probability that somebody

00:51:51.440 --> 00:51:53.090
will add an item to
their order, even

00:51:53.090 --> 00:51:55.040
if it's an inexpensive item.

00:51:55.040 --> 00:51:57.400
So it actually turns out
to work in our interest.

00:51:57.400 --> 00:51:59.090
So what we're
trying to do is, we

00:51:59.090 --> 00:52:01.610
look at-- for
personalization we basically

00:52:01.610 --> 00:52:06.560
calculate for every customer,
a probability that they will--

00:52:06.560 --> 00:52:09.410
what's the most likely
product that you will buy.

00:52:09.410 --> 00:52:13.820
And then we try to take
out the most obvious sort

00:52:13.820 --> 00:52:17.900
of low information content
products like Harry Potter.

00:52:17.900 --> 00:52:20.120
Because probably, you've
already run across that 100

00:52:20.120 --> 00:52:21.950
times in other ways.

00:52:21.950 --> 00:52:23.590
In the supermarket
and everywhere else.

00:52:23.590 --> 00:52:25.216
So putting that in
front of you, it's

00:52:25.216 --> 00:52:28.119
not like you're going to say,
oh Harry Potter 4 is out.

00:52:28.119 --> 00:52:30.035
You know you'd have to
be living under a rock.

00:52:33.540 --> 00:52:35.000
But we try to get
the things that

00:52:35.000 --> 00:52:38.240
are a little bit more
obscure products and then

00:52:38.240 --> 00:52:41.100
within that we optimize
those algorithms.

00:52:41.100 --> 00:52:44.000
And we're certainly doing it
out of our own self-interest.

00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:45.620
I mean we're not--

00:52:45.620 --> 00:52:46.490
there's no question.

00:52:46.490 --> 00:52:48.320
We are in this
business to make money.

00:52:48.320 --> 00:52:50.990
But the way that we
do it, is by trying

00:52:50.990 --> 00:52:53.550
to help customers make
purchase decisions.

00:52:53.550 --> 00:52:55.392
And if you can do that--
and in small ways,

00:52:55.392 --> 00:52:57.350
as I showed you with the
instant order update--

00:52:57.350 --> 00:52:59.308
we've been willing to
accept some features that

00:52:59.308 --> 00:53:00.630
decrease sales.

00:53:00.630 --> 00:53:02.570
But that's because we're
long term oriented.

00:53:02.570 --> 00:53:07.070
We believe that if we give up
those small amounts of sales

00:53:07.070 --> 00:53:10.660
today, the people who are
reminded not to buy the Enya

00:53:10.660 --> 00:53:14.000
CD a second time, will
think that we're so great

00:53:14.000 --> 00:53:16.410
that they'll come back and
buy more in the future.

00:53:16.410 --> 00:53:19.520
So it's a very
simple model, which

00:53:19.520 --> 00:53:23.900
is if you treat customers
right try to do the right thing

00:53:23.900 --> 00:53:26.150
and actually provide real
value, great prices--

00:53:26.150 --> 00:53:29.340
we've been very focused
on cost structure.

00:53:29.340 --> 00:53:32.850
Making the prices-- so that
we can afford to lower prices.

00:53:32.850 --> 00:53:35.990
So we've got free shipping
on orders over $25.

00:53:35.990 --> 00:53:39.380
30% off books over $15.

00:53:39.380 --> 00:53:42.410
Great product prices on
electronics and DVD players

00:53:42.410 --> 00:53:45.110
and digital cameras and so on.

00:53:45.110 --> 00:53:47.720
And we're the only place
you can buy the Segway Human

00:53:47.720 --> 00:53:48.420
Transporter.

00:53:48.420 --> 00:53:50.810
Now I realize this is
a little out of reach

00:53:50.810 --> 00:53:52.880
of your average
undergrad's budget

00:53:52.880 --> 00:53:56.720
but nevertheless, it's
a cool, cool product.

00:53:56.720 --> 00:54:00.850
And then I just like to
offer up my email address.

00:54:00.850 --> 00:54:02.600
I won't answer your
email if you email me,

00:54:02.600 --> 00:54:03.516
but I will forward it.

00:54:03.516 --> 00:54:06.290
I do read my emails and
if anybody is interested,

00:54:06.290 --> 00:54:09.530
we are always
looking for the best

00:54:09.530 --> 00:54:12.710
of the best in terms of
engineering talent, computer

00:54:12.710 --> 00:54:13.910
science talent.

00:54:13.910 --> 00:54:17.910
So if you have any interest
at all know you can email me,

00:54:17.910 --> 00:54:20.960
and I'll ship it off right
away to the right people.

00:54:20.960 --> 00:54:25.520
Anyway with that, I will
end and open it to questions

00:54:25.520 --> 00:54:28.275
and however much time we have,
I'll leave that up to you.

00:54:28.275 --> 00:54:29.150
Who's got a question?

00:54:29.150 --> 00:54:29.651
Yes, please.

00:54:29.651 --> 00:54:31.775
AUDIENCE: There's an
interesting controversy a year

00:54:31.775 --> 00:54:34.150
or two ago, something
about different customers

00:54:34.150 --> 00:54:37.020
were seeing different prices on
the same product [INAUDIBLE]..

00:54:37.020 --> 00:54:37.520
BEZOS: Yeah.

00:54:37.520 --> 00:54:39.228
AUDIENCE: I wanted to
know what going on,

00:54:39.228 --> 00:54:42.740
on the business side of that
with decisions on what to do.

00:54:42.740 --> 00:54:47.440
BEZOS: We did an A/B test
with best selling DVDs.

00:54:47.440 --> 00:54:49.490
Where what we did was, we took--

00:54:49.490 --> 00:54:51.950
what we wanted to
determine was the shape

00:54:51.950 --> 00:54:56.540
of the price elasticity
in best selling DVDs.

00:54:56.540 --> 00:54:59.360
So what we did was, we
randomly took-- we actually

00:54:59.360 --> 00:55:04.130
subdivided customers into
three groups just based

00:55:04.130 --> 00:55:06.780
on the last digit of
their customer ID.

00:55:06.780 --> 00:55:09.200
So it was completely random.

00:55:09.200 --> 00:55:13.340
And we showed some people
30% off, some people 35% off,

00:55:13.340 --> 00:55:15.590
and some people 40% off.

00:55:15.590 --> 00:55:18.230
But what happened is, the
press got this all confused

00:55:18.230 --> 00:55:21.260
and they believed that there
was some devious algorithm where

00:55:21.260 --> 00:55:24.800
we were trying to charge
people in rich zip codes more.

00:55:27.490 --> 00:55:28.756
Which we weren't trying to do.

00:55:28.756 --> 00:55:30.880
We were actually just trying
to determine the shape

00:55:30.880 --> 00:55:32.120
of the elasticity curve.

00:55:32.120 --> 00:55:34.910
But it was so
confusing for people

00:55:34.910 --> 00:55:37.200
that we decided not to run
that kind of experiment.

00:55:37.200 --> 00:55:40.400
So we still do all the A/B tests
but we do them with features.

00:55:40.400 --> 00:55:43.310
We don't do them with prices.

00:55:43.310 --> 00:55:44.085
Yes?

00:55:44.085 --> 00:55:46.251
AUDIENCE: More about
leadership and process question

00:55:46.251 --> 00:55:47.420
on how you organize Amazon.

00:55:47.420 --> 00:55:49.670
Obviously, a lot of the
things that you know

00:55:49.670 --> 00:55:52.010
are very simple basics, like
treat your customers well.

00:55:52.010 --> 00:55:54.051
And then you develop all
these great innovations.

00:55:54.051 --> 00:55:56.649
Now I'm wondering, is it
all internal and customer

00:55:56.649 --> 00:55:58.940
based or do you benchmark
against traditional companies

00:55:58.940 --> 00:56:01.410
and how do you keep this
spirit of innovation alive?

00:56:01.410 --> 00:56:03.365
BEZOS: Well that is a
truly great question.

00:56:05.990 --> 00:56:09.020
First of all, the way to get a
lot of innovation in a company,

00:56:09.020 --> 00:56:12.020
in my opinion is to try
and-- is to work very, very

00:56:12.020 --> 00:56:16.280
hard to reduce the cost
of doing experiments.

00:56:16.280 --> 00:56:20.510
Because the problem is, if
experiments are expensive

00:56:20.510 --> 00:56:22.460
then very few people
are going to get

00:56:22.460 --> 00:56:24.950
to do very few experiments.

00:56:24.950 --> 00:56:26.460
That's just the way it works.

00:56:26.460 --> 00:56:29.840
So if you have a big group
of very creative people--

00:56:29.840 --> 00:56:31.700
which we do, we have
a tremendous number

00:56:31.700 --> 00:56:34.665
of smart people who love to
innovate on behalf of customers

00:56:34.665 --> 00:56:36.572
and invent new things.

00:56:36.572 --> 00:56:38.030
You've got to set
up this kind of--

00:56:38.030 --> 00:56:40.113
like what I was talking
about with the A/B testing

00:56:40.113 --> 00:56:40.820
framework--

00:56:40.820 --> 00:56:42.440
something like that.

00:56:42.440 --> 00:56:45.440
So that if somebody comes up
with a new personalization

00:56:45.440 --> 00:56:48.440
algorithm that they think will
outperform the current one,

00:56:48.440 --> 00:56:53.860
you don't have to move heaven
and earth to get it tried.

00:56:53.860 --> 00:56:57.530
So it's important to reduce
the cost of experimentation

00:56:57.530 --> 00:57:00.140
and then to have some
objective standards

00:57:00.140 --> 00:57:02.240
about what's better than what.

00:57:02.240 --> 00:57:05.670
If you can do that, then you
can do a lot of innovation.

00:57:05.670 --> 00:57:08.420
Now some of the things
that we experiment with

00:57:08.420 --> 00:57:11.750
are really hard to do and
the cost of experimentation

00:57:11.750 --> 00:57:13.400
is high.

00:57:13.400 --> 00:57:18.530
For example, we don't really--
and with consumer behavior,

00:57:18.530 --> 00:57:21.290
it is based-- with
anything totally new,

00:57:21.290 --> 00:57:23.840
it is really very difficult--
anywhere between very difficult

00:57:23.840 --> 00:57:25.220
and impossible--

00:57:25.220 --> 00:57:28.660
to guess how consumers
are going to behave.

00:57:28.660 --> 00:57:31.910
How a mass audience is going
to do anything in the future

00:57:31.910 --> 00:57:33.664
is very hard to determine.

00:57:33.664 --> 00:57:35.330
The easiest way to
determine it, is just

00:57:35.330 --> 00:57:37.484
to do it and see what happens.

00:57:37.484 --> 00:57:39.400
And I think a lot of
companies get that wrong.

00:57:39.400 --> 00:57:42.165
They put too much
energy into like arguing

00:57:42.165 --> 00:57:43.790
about how consumers
are going to behave

00:57:43.790 --> 00:57:45.331
and by the time
they're done arguing,

00:57:45.331 --> 00:57:49.890
they could've just done
it and seen what happened.

00:57:49.890 --> 00:57:53.900
So sometimes-- we spent a
huge amount of effort building

00:57:53.900 --> 00:57:55.910
customer self-service tools.

00:57:55.910 --> 00:57:58.500
So the customers could
cancel their own orders.

00:57:58.500 --> 00:58:02.097
We did some research up front
in the kind of classic way

00:58:02.097 --> 00:58:03.680
to try and determine
whether customers

00:58:03.680 --> 00:58:04.700
would want to do this.

00:58:04.700 --> 00:58:07.880
We got some encouraging results
but focus groups and things

00:58:07.880 --> 00:58:10.750
like that are
fraught with peril.

00:58:10.750 --> 00:58:14.330
People don't know, really
typically, what they will do.

00:58:14.330 --> 00:58:16.356
If you ask them what
you think they'll do,

00:58:16.356 --> 00:58:17.730
they'll try to
tell you the truth

00:58:17.730 --> 00:58:20.010
but they don't really know.

00:58:20.010 --> 00:58:22.340
And so we decided to
just take the gamble.

00:58:22.340 --> 00:58:24.260
There was no way to build
this in a simple way

00:58:24.260 --> 00:58:26.910
and sort of do A/B test
that we could think of.

00:58:26.910 --> 00:58:30.029
So we just went ahead and built
some heavy duty infrastructure

00:58:30.029 --> 00:58:31.820
to allow people to
cancel their own orders.

00:58:31.820 --> 00:58:33.920
By the way, internally
we argued about,

00:58:33.920 --> 00:58:35.900
should we let people
cancel their own orders?

00:58:35.900 --> 00:58:36.950
You know these are
the kinds of things

00:58:36.950 --> 00:58:38.257
that you get cold feet about.

00:58:38.257 --> 00:58:40.340
You're like, maybe they
will cancel their orders--

00:58:40.340 --> 00:58:41.060
[LAUGHTER]

00:58:41.060 --> 00:58:44.096
--and maybe we'll have no sales.

00:58:44.096 --> 00:58:46.820
But in fact, of course, if
you give people more control--

00:58:46.820 --> 00:58:48.903
you know, our basic theory
was, if you give people

00:58:48.903 --> 00:58:50.660
more control over
their environment--

00:58:50.660 --> 00:58:53.180
if they know they can cancel
their own orders then maybe

00:58:53.180 --> 00:58:55.250
they'll order more.

00:58:55.250 --> 00:58:58.100
In fact, that seems
to have been correct.

00:58:58.100 --> 00:59:01.490
That by giving people
these self-service tools,

00:59:01.490 --> 00:59:04.100
we're empowering them and
they seemed to like that.

00:59:04.100 --> 00:59:05.480
But that was an example
where we couldn't really

00:59:05.480 --> 00:59:06.560
do a low cost experiment.

00:59:06.560 --> 00:59:08.601
You kind of had to build
the whole thing in order

00:59:08.601 --> 00:59:10.670
to test it.

00:59:10.670 --> 00:59:11.770
Yes?

00:59:11.770 --> 00:59:16.190
AUDIENCE: There was some other
less than successful experiment

00:59:16.190 --> 00:59:18.980
some time ago,
when companies were

00:59:18.980 --> 00:59:22.592
able to look to see from their
organization what people were

00:59:22.592 --> 00:59:24.380
buying.

00:59:24.380 --> 00:59:27.480
And I'm wondering, with all
of the personalization issues,

00:59:27.480 --> 00:59:30.146
do privacy issues
like that crop up?

00:59:30.146 --> 00:59:31.520
BEZOS: I'm not
sure what you're--

00:59:31.520 --> 00:59:33.860
I think you're referring
to purchase circles.

00:59:33.860 --> 00:59:35.940
Which actually has
been quite successful.

00:59:35.940 --> 00:59:36.824
So I'm not sure.

00:59:36.824 --> 00:59:38.240
AUDIENCE: Before
purchase circles.

00:59:38.240 --> 00:59:39.570
This was a very long time ago.

00:59:39.570 --> 00:59:43.040
There was a news story
I think on Slashdot,

00:59:43.040 --> 00:59:48.087
but Xerox had looked to see
what its employees were buying.

00:59:48.087 --> 00:59:49.670
BEZOS: Yeah but
that's still up there.

00:59:49.670 --> 00:59:50.336
You can do that.

00:59:50.336 --> 00:59:52.400
No, no, no, no, Xerox
can't look to see what

00:59:52.400 --> 00:59:54.010
their employees are buying.

00:59:54.010 --> 00:59:57.650
And I don't-- and I doubt
Slashdot reported that.

00:59:57.650 --> 00:59:58.756
What you're thinking of--

00:59:58.756 --> 01:00:00.380
I know exactly what
you're thinking of.

01:00:00.380 --> 01:00:02.870
Is that we aggregate the data.

01:00:02.870 --> 01:00:06.050
So companies can opt out
if they want but I think,

01:00:06.050 --> 01:00:08.060
like, four companies
have opted out.

01:00:08.060 --> 01:00:09.860
You can go online,
I could show you.

01:00:09.860 --> 01:00:13.490
And you can see what are the
best selling books from people

01:00:13.490 --> 01:00:16.420
who-- at MIT.edu.

01:00:16.420 --> 01:00:18.830
You can go online and see
what are the best selling

01:00:18.830 --> 01:00:24.620
books from amazon.com employees
or from Microsoft employees

01:00:24.620 --> 01:00:25.920
and so on and so on.

01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:27.560
But we have rules
about aggregation

01:00:27.560 --> 01:00:29.250
and we did from
the very beginning.

01:00:29.250 --> 01:00:32.750
So unless the group is
at least 250 customers,

01:00:32.750 --> 01:00:33.750
we don't aggregate that.

01:00:33.750 --> 01:00:35.460
We don't put the data out there.

01:00:35.460 --> 01:00:37.550
I think it's 250--
it's some large number.

01:00:37.550 --> 01:00:40.910
So that people can't back
into who bought what.

01:00:40.910 --> 01:00:43.280
And it turns out to
be really cool data,

01:00:43.280 --> 01:00:45.830
to see what these
companies are buying.

01:00:45.830 --> 01:00:48.440
What the best seller list is.

01:00:48.440 --> 01:00:50.570
We do it geographically
so you can go in

01:00:50.570 --> 01:00:54.979
and look at and see what's
the best selling book in--

01:00:54.979 --> 01:00:57.020
what are the best selling
books in Massachusetts.

01:00:57.020 --> 01:00:58.820
And we do not only
best selling, but we

01:00:58.820 --> 01:01:01.590
do a special algorithm we
call, uniquely best selling.

01:01:01.590 --> 01:01:04.680
Just sort of scrape away all
the Harry Potters and so on.

01:01:04.680 --> 01:01:08.060
So you can see what's uniquely
best selling in Massachusetts.

01:01:08.060 --> 01:01:09.890
What's uniquely best
selling in Boston.

01:01:09.890 --> 01:01:11.865
What's uniquely best
selling in Cambridge.

01:01:11.865 --> 01:01:13.490
What's uniquely best
selling at Harvard

01:01:13.490 --> 01:01:15.510
and what's uniquely
best selling at MIT.

01:01:15.510 --> 01:01:18.930
And you can compare those and
it's actually kind of fun.

01:01:18.930 --> 01:01:21.260
And then we put the best
seller list-- we tie that back

01:01:21.260 --> 01:01:22.485
to the detail pages.

01:01:22.485 --> 01:01:24.860
So if you're stumbling around
and you find a detail page,

01:01:24.860 --> 01:01:28.400
you may see something like, this
is the number seven best seller

01:01:28.400 --> 01:01:31.350
at MIT.edu.

01:01:31.350 --> 01:01:33.840
Anyway, I think that's
what you're thinking of.

01:01:33.840 --> 01:01:34.610
Yeah?

01:01:34.610 --> 01:01:37.400
AUDIENCE: Can you speak back
to the decision you made maybe

01:01:37.400 --> 01:01:40.106
three or four years ago to
expand beyond sell books, DVDs

01:01:40.106 --> 01:01:42.061
and CDs to things like--

01:01:42.061 --> 01:01:43.644
BEZOS: Electronics,
tools and kitchen.

01:01:43.644 --> 01:01:46.168
AUDIENCE: --yeah and one
of the questions, I think,

01:01:46.168 --> 01:01:48.409
of the financial
analysts at Amazon

01:01:48.409 --> 01:01:50.982
would indicate that you don't
make any money off anything

01:01:50.982 --> 01:01:51.646
but books.

01:01:51.646 --> 01:01:53.210
BEZOS: Well, but at that
time we didn't make money

01:01:53.210 --> 01:01:53.990
off books either.

01:01:53.990 --> 01:01:57.410
[LAUGHTER]

01:01:57.410 --> 01:02:01.610
The question was,
why did we expand

01:02:01.610 --> 01:02:04.040
into things like electronics,
tools, and kitchen

01:02:04.040 --> 01:02:07.760
since those businesses are
not, today, profitable?

01:02:07.760 --> 01:02:12.140
And the answer is
that it increases

01:02:12.140 --> 01:02:13.760
our addressable market size.

01:02:13.760 --> 01:02:16.760
So we can ultimately build
a much larger company

01:02:16.760 --> 01:02:19.400
by entering into those
new product categories.

01:02:19.400 --> 01:02:22.997
And second, while it's true
that those new categories, which

01:02:22.997 --> 01:02:25.580
are only three years old-- and
by the way, electronics, tools,

01:02:25.580 --> 01:02:29.270
and kitchen is now a $600
million business at amazon.com.

01:02:29.270 --> 01:02:31.570
Which makes it one
of the largest.

01:02:31.570 --> 01:02:33.530
On a standalone
basis it's probably

01:02:33.530 --> 01:02:36.200
in the top four or five
e-commerce companies

01:02:36.200 --> 01:02:38.010
anywhere in the world.

01:02:38.010 --> 01:02:41.115
So that business is growing
and growing very rapidly.

01:02:41.115 --> 01:02:42.490
One of the big
advantages it has,

01:02:42.490 --> 01:02:44.310
it has a huge audience
of early adopters.

01:02:44.310 --> 01:02:45.726
Which is why
companies like Segway

01:02:45.726 --> 01:02:48.180
choose us as the exclusive
launch platform for products

01:02:48.180 --> 01:02:48.680
like that.

01:02:48.680 --> 01:02:51.110
Because really,
early adopters are

01:02:51.110 --> 01:02:52.910
the kind of people who
buy stuff like this.

01:02:56.820 --> 01:02:58.880
But that business,
ultimately, will

01:02:58.880 --> 01:03:01.110
be-- electronics in
particular, in my opinion,

01:03:01.110 --> 01:03:04.070
will be our largest most
profitable business one day.

01:03:04.070 --> 01:03:05.990
When you start a
new business, there

01:03:05.990 --> 01:03:07.670
are certain fixed
costs that come along

01:03:07.670 --> 01:03:08.836
with starting that business.

01:03:08.836 --> 01:03:11.510
And then the business has to
reach enough scale so that

01:03:11.510 --> 01:03:14.420
the profit from the business--
the variable profit--

01:03:14.420 --> 01:03:16.440
can cover those fixed costs.

01:03:16.440 --> 01:03:17.720
There's no sidestepping that.

01:03:17.720 --> 01:03:20.340
It's just a simple
investment issue.

01:03:20.340 --> 01:03:23.120
The company now, in a
trailing 12 month basis,

01:03:23.120 --> 01:03:25.820
generated $120 million
in free cash flow

01:03:25.820 --> 01:03:28.670
after interest expense.

01:03:28.670 --> 01:03:31.220
And really we're
just at break-even.

01:03:31.220 --> 01:03:33.860
The company is doing
very well financially.

01:03:33.860 --> 01:03:34.900
Yeah, way in the back.

01:03:34.900 --> 01:03:36.316
AUDIENCE: A little
bit about-- you

01:03:36.316 --> 01:03:38.107
were talking about
investing in technology.

01:03:38.107 --> 01:03:40.160
How do you deal with
competitors, like Barnes

01:03:40.160 --> 01:03:40.796
and Noble, for example.

01:03:40.796 --> 01:03:42.620
They've got that new
book browsing thing.

01:03:42.620 --> 01:03:44.990
How you deal with the fact that
new technologies are going out

01:03:44.990 --> 01:03:46.520
there and how you
compete against that,

01:03:46.520 --> 01:03:48.103
despite the fact
that you guys already

01:03:48.103 --> 01:03:49.430
invest a lot internally?

01:03:49.430 --> 01:03:50.900
BEZOS: Well really that is the--

01:03:50.900 --> 01:03:54.440
I mean the good news, from a
technology investment point

01:03:54.440 --> 01:03:58.970
of view, is that compared
to most of our competitors--

01:03:58.970 --> 01:04:01.370
well actually, really,
I guess compared to all

01:04:01.370 --> 01:04:02.850
of our competitors--

01:04:02.850 --> 01:04:04.240
we're spending so much more.

01:04:04.240 --> 01:04:07.580
You would probably have to
add up the next 10 e-tailers

01:04:07.580 --> 01:04:12.010
together to get anywhere near
our technology spending budget.

01:04:12.010 --> 01:04:14.325
So at $200 million a year--

01:04:14.325 --> 01:04:15.700
you know, most of
our competitors

01:04:15.700 --> 01:04:18.590
don't have $200
million in sales.

01:04:18.590 --> 01:04:19.600
So they can't spend--

01:04:19.600 --> 01:04:22.030
we have $4 billion in sales.

01:04:22.030 --> 01:04:27.150
So $200 million a year
is just 5% of sales.

01:04:27.150 --> 01:04:32.500
At BarnesandNoble.com, $200
million would be half of sales.

01:04:32.500 --> 01:04:35.834
So that's a significant
advantage for us

01:04:35.834 --> 01:04:37.000
as we continue to build out.

01:04:37.000 --> 01:04:40.060
We want the gap between
our customer experience

01:04:40.060 --> 01:04:42.850
and those of our competitors
to actually widen.

01:04:42.850 --> 01:04:46.360
With these kinds of
personalization features.

01:04:46.360 --> 01:04:49.660
There's lots of stuff that I
can't talk about on the drawing

01:04:49.660 --> 01:04:53.140
board, that we think is going to
make the experience much, much

01:04:53.140 --> 01:04:54.340
better in the future.

01:04:54.340 --> 01:04:57.310
AUDIENCE: $200 million,
how much of it's R&amp;D?

01:04:57.310 --> 01:04:59.710
BEZOS: Well, we-- the question
was, of that $200 million,

01:04:59.710 --> 01:05:01.490
how much of it is R&amp;D?

01:05:01.490 --> 01:05:03.651
I never know exactly how
to answer that question.

01:05:03.651 --> 01:05:04.150
I mean--

01:05:04.150 --> 01:05:05.640
AUDIENCE: Capital as
opposed to disk drives.

01:05:05.640 --> 01:05:07.181
BEZOS: Oh, as opposed
to disk drives.

01:05:07.181 --> 01:05:08.080
Almost all of it is--

01:05:08.080 --> 01:05:10.510
I mean there's very little.

01:05:10.510 --> 01:05:14.400
The good news is, we're
a total Linux platform.

01:05:14.400 --> 01:05:15.590
So it's very cheap.

01:05:15.590 --> 01:05:17.290
It's all commodity hardware.

01:05:17.290 --> 01:05:20.770
We have a few big
database servers.

01:05:20.770 --> 01:05:22.850
But the stuff has
gotten so cheap.

01:05:22.850 --> 01:05:26.010
I don't know exactly what the
fraction is but a lot of--

01:05:26.010 --> 01:05:30.360
a big fraction of that
expense is people expense.

01:05:30.360 --> 01:05:32.720
It's, you know, smart people.

01:05:32.720 --> 01:05:33.727
Yeah?

01:05:33.727 --> 01:05:35.185
AUDIENCE: Who are
your competitors?

01:05:35.185 --> 01:05:39.080
In your state, now where
Amazon is, right now?

01:05:39.080 --> 01:05:42.610
BEZOS: Well there are two
groups of competitors.

01:05:42.610 --> 01:05:44.780
Our big competition is
in the physical world.

01:05:47.330 --> 01:05:50.120
Online is still so small.

01:05:50.120 --> 01:05:55.940
Retail sales worldwide are
a $5 trillion dollar market.

01:05:55.940 --> 01:05:58.310
Online is less than
a percent of that.

01:05:58.310 --> 01:06:01.220
It's very, very tiny today.

01:06:01.220 --> 01:06:04.640
So you don't want to take sales
away from online competitors

01:06:04.640 --> 01:06:08.290
just because there isn't
that much sales to take away.

01:06:08.290 --> 01:06:10.040
So if you look at
physical-- all the sales

01:06:10.040 --> 01:06:10.860
in the physical world.

01:06:10.860 --> 01:06:12.080
So that's where the dollars are.

01:06:12.080 --> 01:06:14.390
That's where you need to
focus your primary competitive

01:06:14.390 --> 01:06:16.400
energy.

01:06:16.400 --> 01:06:18.747
You look at online
companies not because you

01:06:18.747 --> 01:06:21.080
want to get their sales, but
because sometimes you might

01:06:21.080 --> 01:06:22.204
be able to learn from them.

01:06:22.204 --> 01:06:25.142
I mean they're watching
customers too, hopefully.

01:06:25.142 --> 01:06:26.600
And so you need to
see what they're

01:06:26.600 --> 01:06:27.891
doing, how are they innovating?

01:06:27.891 --> 01:06:28.975
What are they thinking of?

01:06:28.975 --> 01:06:30.765
What kind of lessons
can we learn from that

01:06:30.765 --> 01:06:33.170
and incorporate into doing
things the way we do things?

01:06:33.170 --> 01:06:35.810
Rarely, by the way, have
I seen it successful

01:06:35.810 --> 01:06:37.850
online to do direct
copies of things.

01:06:37.850 --> 01:06:40.490
Usually, you get some
inspiration from something

01:06:40.490 --> 01:06:43.250
but then you have
to make it your own

01:06:43.250 --> 01:06:47.720
and make it be part of
doing things the way

01:06:47.720 --> 01:06:49.190
we do things for our customers.

01:06:51.944 --> 01:06:53.360
Once you're in the
physical world,

01:06:53.360 --> 01:06:55.235
the big competitors--
there's a different one

01:06:55.235 --> 01:06:56.210
for every category.

01:06:56.210 --> 01:06:59.420
So we would look at
Best Buy in electronics

01:06:59.420 --> 01:07:00.680
as our top competitor.

01:07:00.680 --> 01:07:02.090
Best Buy in the physical world.

01:07:02.090 --> 01:07:03.674
Barnes and Noble in books.

01:07:03.674 --> 01:07:05.090
And you can go
right down the line

01:07:05.090 --> 01:07:07.020
in every category and you can--

01:07:07.020 --> 01:07:12.710
it's usually the kind of the
selection intensive superstore

01:07:12.710 --> 01:07:16.780
type competitor
defined by category.

01:07:16.780 --> 01:07:17.280
Yes?

01:07:17.280 --> 01:07:20.150
AUDIENCE: I've got a "as far as
the business of web services,"

01:07:20.150 --> 01:07:20.651
question.

01:07:20.651 --> 01:07:23.191
I've got to imagine that there's
a fair amount of money to be

01:07:23.191 --> 01:07:25.810
made from the information
that Amazon generates and is

01:07:25.810 --> 01:07:27.101
able to collect from consumers.

01:07:27.101 --> 01:07:30.950
For example, I know many
product designers for baby toys

01:07:30.950 --> 01:07:33.210
go to Amazon, they look
up their customer reviews

01:07:33.210 --> 01:07:35.350
and figure out how
people feel about it.

01:07:35.350 --> 01:07:37.500
I'm wondering, so now
if I were to start

01:07:37.500 --> 01:07:40.330
using the web services from
Amazon to try to resell it,

01:07:40.330 --> 01:07:41.539
repackage it.

01:07:41.539 --> 01:07:42.580
How would you guys react?

01:07:42.580 --> 01:07:44.890
Sort of what's that
interplay like?

01:07:44.890 --> 01:07:46.900
BEZOS: The answer
is, right now you

01:07:46.900 --> 01:07:49.450
have to try and use the
web services as part

01:07:49.450 --> 01:07:51.970
of our Associates Program.

01:07:51.970 --> 01:07:55.240
So there's a contract
you sign when you sign up

01:07:55.240 --> 01:07:57.100
to get a developer token.

01:07:57.100 --> 01:07:59.030
There's some rules.

01:07:59.030 --> 01:08:02.530
And one of the things you
cannot do is download the data

01:08:02.530 --> 01:08:05.290
and repackage it for the sort
of purposes that you're talking

01:08:05.290 --> 01:08:06.510
about.

01:08:06.510 --> 01:08:08.700
And you can't cache the data.

01:08:08.700 --> 01:08:10.360
There are several things that--

01:08:10.360 --> 01:08:12.052
because the data is valuable.

01:08:12.052 --> 01:08:13.760
We don't want you just
to write a program

01:08:13.760 --> 01:08:17.689
that like sucks all the data
out and then it's your data.

01:08:17.689 --> 01:08:19.490
That can't be.

01:08:19.490 --> 01:08:21.622
But you do-- but
if you're playing

01:08:21.622 --> 01:08:23.080
by the rules of
being an associate,

01:08:23.080 --> 01:08:25.330
you can invent a
lot of cool stuff.

01:08:25.330 --> 01:08:27.069
You own that stuff.

01:08:27.069 --> 01:08:29.890
You read the contract, you own
your application and the things

01:08:29.890 --> 01:08:31.569
you build on top of it.

01:08:31.569 --> 01:08:37.380
We just reserve the right
to take the data back.

01:08:37.380 --> 01:08:37.880
Yeah?

01:08:37.880 --> 01:08:39.700
We'll do some more
down here in front.

01:08:39.700 --> 01:08:42.050
AUDIENCE: So looking to
the non-desktop world--

01:08:42.050 --> 01:08:43.529
what I guess you
could define it--

01:08:43.529 --> 01:08:44.580
BEZOS: The non which world?

01:08:44.580 --> 01:08:45.390
AUDIENCE: The non-desktop.

01:08:45.390 --> 01:08:46.181
BEZOS: Non-desktop.

01:08:46.181 --> 01:08:48.759
AUDIENCE: Mobile or wireless.

01:08:48.759 --> 01:08:50.300
From a technology
perspective, what's

01:08:50.300 --> 01:08:53.080
uniquely interesting
about that world,

01:08:53.080 --> 01:08:54.972
that makes you want to
keep pumping dollars--

01:08:54.972 --> 01:08:55.930
or any dollars into it.

01:08:55.930 --> 01:08:58.080
Or, perhaps is it not unique.

01:08:58.080 --> 01:09:02.051
AUDIENCE: So the question is,
is the mobile wireless world

01:09:02.051 --> 01:09:02.550
interesting?

01:09:02.550 --> 01:09:04.800
AUDIENCE: Aside from the
presentation-- combining lots

01:09:04.800 --> 01:09:06.169
of things into a small screen.

01:09:06.169 --> 01:09:06.710
BEZOS: Right.

01:09:09.674 --> 01:09:13.130
I think the answer is,
that that world is not

01:09:13.130 --> 01:09:17.359
very interesting today,
with the exception of Japan.

01:09:17.359 --> 01:09:20.060
Where, for whatever reason, it's
worked a lot better and people

01:09:20.060 --> 01:09:22.069
are willing to--

01:09:22.069 --> 01:09:24.120
the real problem is the devices.

01:09:24.120 --> 01:09:26.300
So the devices have
very small input device

01:09:26.300 --> 01:09:28.279
and very small output device.

01:09:28.279 --> 01:09:32.660
And as we get to see
better web pads--

01:09:32.660 --> 01:09:36.020
like this is a great
device, the BlackBerry.

01:09:36.020 --> 01:09:39.460
For doing email this
is my most valuable--

01:09:39.460 --> 01:09:42.350
I'd give up my cell phone before
I would give up my BlackBerry.

01:09:45.350 --> 01:09:48.890
You will see devices with
this sort of form factor,

01:09:48.890 --> 01:09:53.510
with decent input devices,
that have a decent color

01:09:53.510 --> 01:09:56.540
screens with enough
resolution so you can actually

01:09:56.540 --> 01:10:00.230
look at enough data to actually
do something on the web.

01:10:00.230 --> 01:10:03.440
Right now, if you're
using your cell phone

01:10:03.440 --> 01:10:06.710
to do things on the
web, you're like hitting

01:10:06.710 --> 01:10:08.980
the three key three times
to get a particular--

01:10:08.980 --> 01:10:10.220
I mean it's just bad.

01:10:10.220 --> 01:10:12.120
It's a bad customer experience.

01:10:12.120 --> 01:10:15.840
It's the best you can do perhaps
with that physical device.

01:10:15.840 --> 01:10:17.572
But that stuff will get better.

01:10:17.572 --> 01:10:18.780
And that's a pretty big deal.

01:10:18.780 --> 01:10:20.904
The thing that's a bigger
deal in the shorter term,

01:10:20.904 --> 01:10:23.000
is more people getting
instant on at home.

01:10:23.000 --> 01:10:26.160
It's not even the extra
bandwidth that you get at home.

01:10:26.160 --> 01:10:28.460
It's the fact that you can
leave your computer on.

01:10:28.460 --> 01:10:30.269
I think the thing
that will happen next,

01:10:30.269 --> 01:10:32.060
is that people will
have multiple computers

01:10:32.060 --> 01:10:32.990
in their home.

01:10:32.990 --> 01:10:35.180
So I already see that
starting to happen.

01:10:35.180 --> 01:10:37.430
One of our best selling
electronics products for years

01:10:37.430 --> 01:10:39.440
has been wireless routers.

01:10:39.440 --> 01:10:44.300
A lot of people installing
802.11 networks in their home.

01:10:44.300 --> 01:10:47.330
And it completely
changes your behavior

01:10:47.330 --> 01:10:50.230
when you get two or three or
four computers in your house

01:10:50.230 --> 01:10:53.330
instead of through one in the
office or one in the bedroom

01:10:53.330 --> 01:10:54.080
or whatever it is.

01:10:54.080 --> 01:10:57.260
When you look at your
average household,

01:10:57.260 --> 01:10:59.990
you're going to start to see
that the number of computer--

01:10:59.990 --> 01:11:03.200
just like with phones, there
was a time when people said,

01:11:03.200 --> 01:11:05.960
why, would anybody ever have two
phones in their house, that's

01:11:05.960 --> 01:11:07.850
sort of ridiculous.

01:11:07.850 --> 01:11:09.477
Absurd luxury.

01:11:09.477 --> 01:11:11.810
And now, of course, people
put a phone in every bedroom.

01:11:11.810 --> 01:11:13.220
They put them in
their bathrooms.

01:11:13.220 --> 01:11:14.553
They put them in their kitchens.

01:11:14.553 --> 01:11:15.830
They put them everywhere.

01:11:15.830 --> 01:11:18.110
And the kitchen is
actually the key room.

01:11:18.110 --> 01:11:20.870
So I put a computer
in my kitchen

01:11:20.870 --> 01:11:22.730
and it doubled my
amazon.com purchases.

01:11:22.730 --> 01:11:26.530
[LAUGHTER]

01:11:26.530 --> 01:11:28.445
I strongly recommend--
you need an instant

01:11:28.445 --> 01:11:30.290
on computer in your kitchen.

01:11:30.290 --> 01:11:31.539
That will be a big deal.

01:11:31.539 --> 01:11:33.830
AUDIENCE: Is your behavior,
when you're in the kitchen,

01:11:33.830 --> 01:11:36.350
different enough that
amazon.com would take that

01:11:36.350 --> 01:11:38.960
into consideration in
making your recommendations?

01:11:38.960 --> 01:11:40.670
BEZOS: By making
kitchen recommendations?

01:11:40.670 --> 01:11:43.940
[LAUGHTER]

01:11:43.940 --> 01:11:46.790
I hadn't thought about that.

01:11:46.790 --> 01:11:48.511
Recommendations by room.

01:11:48.511 --> 01:11:49.010
Right.

01:11:49.010 --> 01:11:51.080
We need to be able to
impute your room though.

01:11:51.080 --> 01:11:53.840
Otherwise we'd have to ask
people, what room are you in?

01:11:53.840 --> 01:11:55.790
Or have you like
register your computer.

01:11:55.790 --> 01:11:57.580
This is my kitchen computer.

01:11:57.580 --> 01:11:58.340
[LAUGHTER]

01:11:58.340 --> 01:12:02.120
We'll make the OXO salad
spinner recommendation in there.

01:12:02.120 --> 01:12:02.782
Yes?

01:12:02.782 --> 01:12:04.490
AUDIENCE: A while ago
there was a dispute

01:12:04.490 --> 01:12:08.630
over the Amazon policy on
patents, on business methods.

01:12:08.630 --> 01:12:09.130
BEZOS: Yeah.

01:12:09.130 --> 01:12:09.415
AUDIENCE: What's
your feeling on that?

01:12:09.415 --> 01:12:11.920
Because there was some
replication and I believe

01:12:11.920 --> 01:12:14.390
you made a statement that you
thought that it was bad news.

01:12:14.390 --> 01:12:16.670
BEZOS: What I think
is-- my position on this

01:12:16.670 --> 01:12:20.330
is very clear which is, I
think patents in general

01:12:20.330 --> 01:12:22.880
are really, very good.

01:12:22.880 --> 01:12:25.700
If you look at the history
of the patent office

01:12:25.700 --> 01:12:27.320
and of innovation
in this country,

01:12:27.320 --> 01:12:30.340
it's hard to unlink the two.

01:12:30.340 --> 01:12:32.330
Now one of the
things that I think

01:12:32.330 --> 01:12:34.290
is-- if you look
back historically,

01:12:34.290 --> 01:12:36.250
the rate of change was slower.

01:12:36.250 --> 01:12:38.000
So if you look at the
lifetime of patents,

01:12:38.000 --> 01:12:39.420
it's actually increased
a little bit because

01:12:39.420 --> 01:12:41.510
of some international
treaties and it's basically

01:12:41.510 --> 01:12:45.320
gone from 17 years to 20 years.

01:12:45.320 --> 01:12:47.210
And for some
industries, 20 years

01:12:47.210 --> 01:12:48.870
is very appropriate lifetime.

01:12:48.870 --> 01:12:51.105
So for example, if
you're manufacturing

01:12:51.105 --> 01:12:54.890
a new pharmaceutical,
you probably need--

01:12:54.890 --> 01:12:56.690
you may need 20 years.

01:12:56.690 --> 01:12:59.090
You've got to go through
10 years of FDA testing,

01:12:59.090 --> 01:13:01.370
and there's all
this stuff going on.

01:13:01.370 --> 01:13:05.400
You may need a long lifetime
to recoup your R&amp;D costs.

01:13:05.400 --> 01:13:07.550
And you need some
exclusive period

01:13:07.550 --> 01:13:11.380
where all that R&amp;D
benefits only you.

01:13:11.380 --> 01:13:13.940
But when you look
at software patents,

01:13:13.940 --> 01:13:17.240
it seems to me that 20
years may be too long.

01:13:17.240 --> 01:13:20.376
And so that's the point I made.

01:13:20.376 --> 01:13:21.500
It's really the same thing.

01:13:24.440 --> 01:13:30.060
I would say that there is
no chance of that changing.

01:13:30.060 --> 01:13:32.450
And the reason is, when
you go look at this--

01:13:32.450 --> 01:13:34.162
and I did, I actually
went to Congress

01:13:34.162 --> 01:13:36.120
and I made my case in
front of few people, that

01:13:36.120 --> 01:13:37.850
seemed like too long a period.

01:13:37.850 --> 01:13:41.740
And the problem is, this thing
is now so well established,

01:13:41.740 --> 01:13:45.160
not just in the US but
by international treaty.

01:13:45.160 --> 01:13:48.220
Literally, hundreds of
nations that have all

01:13:48.220 --> 01:13:52.780
entered into mutually binding
patent treaties and it's

01:13:52.780 --> 01:13:56.660
very hard to get that
whole web of stuff changed.

01:13:56.660 --> 01:13:58.000
So that's why it's--

01:13:58.000 --> 01:14:00.380
people tend to leave it alone.

01:14:00.380 --> 01:14:01.290
Yeah.

01:14:01.290 --> 01:14:03.430
AUDIENCE: So
getting stuff to me.

01:14:03.430 --> 01:14:05.070
It seems like your
business will scale,

01:14:05.070 --> 01:14:09.490
your computers will
scale, but one roadblock

01:14:09.490 --> 01:14:12.210
between me buying stuff
on Amazon all the time,

01:14:12.210 --> 01:14:16.690
is getting that stuff from
Washington to my home.

01:14:16.690 --> 01:14:19.729
I applaud you all for making
that $25 free shipping stuff.

01:14:19.729 --> 01:14:21.770
I think it's amazing, I
don't know how you do it.

01:14:21.770 --> 01:14:23.520
But where do you think
that's going to go?

01:14:23.520 --> 01:14:26.194
How do you think
those costs scale?

01:14:26.194 --> 01:14:27.610
BEZOS: Well it's
a great question.

01:14:27.610 --> 01:14:29.140
The question
basically is, is there

01:14:29.140 --> 01:14:35.800
some way to dramatically
change the delivery

01:14:35.800 --> 01:14:39.310
part of the business
model, so that you are--

01:14:39.310 --> 01:14:42.100
and the answer is, at
certain scale levels, maybe.

01:14:42.100 --> 01:14:44.720
So already it's changed a lot
because when we first started,

01:14:44.720 --> 01:14:46.570
we shipped only out of Seattle.

01:14:46.570 --> 01:14:47.560
Now we have--

01:14:47.560 --> 01:14:49.060
AUDIENCE: Fine, but
you're not going

01:14:49.060 --> 01:14:50.230
to have one in every city.

01:14:50.230 --> 01:14:53.667
BEZOS: Well, why not?

01:14:53.667 --> 01:14:56.000
AUDIENCE: Do you get the cost
benefit from that, though?

01:14:56.000 --> 01:14:57.590
BEZOS: Well it's all
a question of scale.

01:14:57.590 --> 01:14:58.520
If the question was,
you're not going

01:14:58.520 --> 01:15:00.620
to have one in every
city, then you probably

01:15:00.620 --> 01:15:02.420
won't have one in every city.

01:15:02.420 --> 01:15:04.130
But you very well
might have one--

01:15:04.130 --> 01:15:05.630
I mean, you need a lot of scale.

01:15:05.630 --> 01:15:07.400
At $4 billion in
scale, we've got

01:15:07.400 --> 01:15:09.890
the right number of
fulfillment centers right now.

01:15:09.890 --> 01:15:13.370
But if you take that from
$4 billion to $40 billion,

01:15:13.370 --> 01:15:15.497
things would start to
change significantly.

01:15:15.497 --> 01:15:17.455
AUDIENCE: You actually
don't want a fulfillment

01:15:17.455 --> 01:15:20.180
in Massachusetts because then
you pay shipping and taxes.

01:15:20.180 --> 01:15:22.346
BEZOS: Yeah, you'd probably
put it in New Hampshire.

01:15:22.346 --> 01:15:24.664
[LAUGHTER]

01:15:24.664 --> 01:15:27.470
AUDIENCE: How do you
do that $25 deal?

01:15:27.470 --> 01:15:30.540
Do you get-- how does that work?

01:15:30.540 --> 01:15:31.890
No one else does it.

01:15:31.890 --> 01:15:34.150
BEZOS: Well, that's why you
should shop at amazon.com.

01:15:34.150 --> 01:15:37.482
I like this guy.

01:15:37.482 --> 01:15:39.440
Hey you're looking
particularly handsome today.

01:15:39.440 --> 01:15:41.960
[LAUGHTER]

01:15:44.090 --> 01:15:47.090
It is-- the $25
Super Saver Shipping

01:15:47.090 --> 01:15:49.410
is incredibly expensive.

01:15:49.410 --> 01:15:51.490
So it has to be--

01:15:51.490 --> 01:15:54.020
it's the most expensive price
reduction we've ever made.

01:15:54.020 --> 01:15:55.490
And we're still hopeful,
by the way, that we'll

01:15:55.490 --> 01:15:56.630
leave it in place permanently.

01:15:56.630 --> 01:15:58.338
We're going to make
a decision at the end

01:15:58.338 --> 01:16:00.770
of the year about whether
to make it permanent.

01:16:00.770 --> 01:16:01.670
We were at $49.

01:16:01.670 --> 01:16:04.880
If we can't make $25 permanent,
we'll go back to $49.

01:16:04.880 --> 01:16:08.270
But I'm very hopeful we can
make the $25 level permanent.

01:16:08.270 --> 01:16:13.370
And the key there, is
being very efficient.

01:16:13.370 --> 01:16:16.040
The economics can
be made to work.

01:16:16.040 --> 01:16:18.830
It's just a question
of, does it generate

01:16:18.830 --> 01:16:20.750
enough incremental
lift in volume

01:16:20.750 --> 01:16:24.200
to justify the huge
price reduction

01:16:24.200 --> 01:16:26.330
that it effectively is.

01:16:26.330 --> 01:16:27.380
Yes?

01:16:27.380 --> 01:16:29.780
AUDIENCE: If you were
a 2002 college graduate

01:16:29.780 --> 01:16:31.611
with your newly minted
engineering degree,

01:16:31.611 --> 01:16:33.110
what are some of
the fields you'd be

01:16:33.110 --> 01:16:35.270
most interested in working in?

01:16:35.270 --> 01:16:39.159
BEZOS: Newly minted
2002 college student

01:16:39.159 --> 01:16:41.450
with an engineering degree,
what are some of the fields

01:16:41.450 --> 01:16:44.750
that I would be most
interested in working in?

01:16:44.750 --> 01:16:50.810
Well first of all,
what I would say is--

01:16:50.810 --> 01:16:52.880
the best advice I
can give somebody,

01:16:52.880 --> 01:16:55.820
is do something that you
think is interesting.

01:16:55.820 --> 01:16:58.269
And let the waves catch you.

01:16:58.269 --> 01:16:59.810
Something that I
see people do, which

01:16:59.810 --> 01:17:02.150
I don't think is a
good idea, is they

01:17:02.150 --> 01:17:03.530
try to chase the current thing.

01:17:03.530 --> 01:17:08.270
I saw so much in
1997 and 1998, 1999,

01:17:08.270 --> 01:17:09.980
with respect to the internet.

01:17:09.980 --> 01:17:17.720
There were-- in '94, '95, '96,
and '97, mostly the people who

01:17:17.720 --> 01:17:18.944
were--

01:17:18.944 --> 01:17:20.360
the earlier you
get, the more true

01:17:20.360 --> 01:17:23.720
this is-- but the people who are
sort of looking at this space

01:17:23.720 --> 01:17:25.970
and started companies were
genuinely interested in it.

01:17:25.970 --> 01:17:28.090
They thought it was really cool.

01:17:28.090 --> 01:17:31.850
By the time you got
to 1999, doctors

01:17:31.850 --> 01:17:35.430
were stopping-- you know,
screw that doctor thing,

01:17:35.430 --> 01:17:38.150
I'm going to start
a dot com company!

01:17:38.150 --> 01:17:42.144
So that really made
no sense whatsoever.

01:17:42.144 --> 01:17:44.060
People were sort of
abandoning the things they

01:17:44.060 --> 01:17:46.268
were genuinely interested
and trying to catch a wave.

01:17:46.268 --> 01:17:47.740
Whenever you try
to catch a wave,

01:17:47.740 --> 01:17:50.540
you're almost always too late.

01:17:50.540 --> 01:17:52.040
So it's better to just--

01:17:52.040 --> 01:17:53.540
I mean, if you're
paddling after it.

01:17:53.540 --> 01:17:56.742
You basically have to wait in
place and let it come to you.

01:17:56.742 --> 01:17:58.950
So just bring something that
you're passionate about.

01:17:58.950 --> 01:18:01.670
I would definitely-- on
the career advice side,

01:18:01.670 --> 01:18:06.230
the most important thing about
your first job out of school

01:18:06.230 --> 01:18:09.950
is to pick a place where
your learning per unit time

01:18:09.950 --> 01:18:11.420
is going to be very high.

01:18:11.420 --> 01:18:13.580
You can optimize for
a bunch of variables

01:18:13.580 --> 01:18:14.930
and it can get very confusing.

01:18:14.930 --> 01:18:19.150
You know, where's the location,
what's the salary going to be?

01:18:19.150 --> 01:18:20.900
And all those things
are worth considering

01:18:20.900 --> 01:18:21.983
and they're all important.

01:18:21.983 --> 01:18:24.500
But make sure you pull back
after you've done your analysis

01:18:24.500 --> 01:18:26.960
and say, am I also
picking the place

01:18:26.960 --> 01:18:28.670
that I believe my
learning per unit time

01:18:28.670 --> 01:18:30.890
is going to be the fastest.

01:18:30.890 --> 01:18:33.252
You learn much more from
a best practices company

01:18:33.252 --> 01:18:35.210
than you do-- you learn
no matter where you go,

01:18:35.210 --> 01:18:36.830
so if you go work for
somebody and you're like,

01:18:36.830 --> 01:18:38.690
these people are all
crazy, this is insane--

01:18:38.690 --> 01:18:42.260
you'll learn a lot of negative
lessons that you can use later

01:18:42.260 --> 01:18:44.090
and you can not do those things.

01:18:44.090 --> 01:18:48.480
But that process of elimination
method takes a long time.

01:18:48.480 --> 01:18:50.480
So it's better to try
and find a place that has

01:18:50.480 --> 01:18:51.900
really high hiring standards.

01:18:51.900 --> 01:18:53.220
Some of the basics especially.

01:18:53.220 --> 01:18:57.410
You know, a place that's trying
to recruit only the you know--

01:18:57.410 --> 01:19:01.730
A's hire A's and B's hire
C's and C's hire D's.

01:19:01.730 --> 01:19:03.925
So you got to hire only A's.

01:19:03.925 --> 01:19:05.300
You need to pick
a company that's

01:19:05.300 --> 01:19:07.490
doing those kinds of
fundamental practices

01:19:07.490 --> 01:19:10.130
well, because the you'll learn
so much that will set you up

01:19:10.130 --> 01:19:11.700
to do other things.

01:19:11.700 --> 01:19:14.060
So I would say that's
probably the best career

01:19:14.060 --> 01:19:15.044
advice I can give.

01:19:15.044 --> 01:19:16.460
HOST: We've got
time for one more.

01:19:16.460 --> 01:19:17.876
BEZOS: Time for
one more question?

01:19:17.876 --> 01:19:18.497
Okay.

01:19:18.497 --> 01:19:20.830
AUDIENCE: I was wondering
about the international sites.

01:19:20.830 --> 01:19:25.172
I was actually buying stuff from
different sites like Canada, US

01:19:25.172 --> 01:19:26.109
and Japan.

01:19:26.109 --> 01:19:28.525
I've noticed that these sites
are actually really similar.

01:19:28.525 --> 01:19:30.520
BEZOS: The site are similar.

01:19:30.520 --> 01:19:32.721
Yeah our international
sites are similar.

01:19:32.721 --> 01:19:35.520
AUDIENCE: I was
wondering, how do

01:19:35.520 --> 01:19:37.720
you manage your
international subsidiaries?

01:19:37.720 --> 01:19:40.770
BEZOS: It's a very
good question and I'm

01:19:40.770 --> 01:19:42.660
very pleased to
note that you have

01:19:42.660 --> 01:19:45.840
found the sites to be similar.

01:19:45.840 --> 01:19:50.640
We insist-- all of our software
development is done in Seattle.

01:19:50.640 --> 01:19:53.160
We don't allow any software
development to be done--

01:19:53.160 --> 01:19:56.370
we do have big physical
operations and great marketing

01:19:56.370 --> 01:19:59.010
teams and so on
outside of the US.

01:19:59.010 --> 01:20:03.090
But the software is so core
and so expensive to do well,

01:20:03.090 --> 01:20:06.090
that you want to have exactly
one set of source code

01:20:06.090 --> 01:20:08.970
that gets used all over
the world, globally.

01:20:08.970 --> 01:20:12.720
And many companies
have made the mistake

01:20:12.720 --> 01:20:14.854
of allowing their
different sets of source

01:20:14.854 --> 01:20:16.770
code in different countries
to get out of sync

01:20:16.770 --> 01:20:19.030
and they end up with two
completely different systems.

01:20:19.030 --> 01:20:21.240
Now they're getting
no fixed cost leverage

01:20:21.240 --> 01:20:22.509
across those two systems.

01:20:22.509 --> 01:20:24.300
They've got two different
teams, developing

01:20:24.300 --> 01:20:25.514
two different systems.

01:20:25.514 --> 01:20:27.930
And then they decide one day,
this is terrible and stupid,

01:20:27.930 --> 01:20:29.410
how did we let this happen?

01:20:29.410 --> 01:20:31.500
And they want to
recombine those systems.

01:20:31.500 --> 01:20:33.970
And the systems by that point
have diverged so much that

01:20:33.970 --> 01:20:36.910
recombining them
is very difficult.

01:20:36.910 --> 01:20:41.040
So it's very important to have
one set of source code around

01:20:41.040 --> 01:20:43.504
the world and that's--

01:20:43.504 --> 01:20:44.670
thank you for noticing that.

01:20:44.670 --> 01:20:48.710
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
distribution [INAUDIBLE]..

01:20:48.710 --> 01:20:53.160
BEZOS: But the software that
runs the fulfillment centers

01:20:53.160 --> 01:20:55.610
is also the same
all over the world.

01:20:55.610 --> 01:20:56.790
So the key is--

01:20:56.790 --> 01:20:58.390
you know, what
happens to software.

01:20:58.390 --> 01:21:00.440
Software is fascinating
in a business

01:21:00.440 --> 01:21:04.660
because not only do you do
all the things that you're

01:21:04.660 --> 01:21:07.150
trying to do, but some stuff--

01:21:07.150 --> 01:21:10.810
what happens is
business knowledge gets,

01:21:10.810 --> 01:21:13.840
over time, embedded
in the software.

01:21:13.840 --> 01:21:16.900
So little bits and pieces--
the software ends up

01:21:16.900 --> 01:21:18.820
knowing-- this is
my opinion, again

01:21:18.820 --> 01:21:22.480
I admit to being sort
of computer centric.

01:21:22.480 --> 01:21:25.360
But in my opinion, at
the end of the day,

01:21:25.360 --> 01:21:29.120
the software knows more about
your business than you do.

01:21:29.120 --> 01:21:31.750
And so it's really
important that you only

01:21:31.750 --> 01:21:33.100
have one such system.

01:21:33.100 --> 01:21:34.660
Because it's going
to be complicated

01:21:34.660 --> 01:21:37.030
and it's going to
be very interesting.

01:21:37.030 --> 01:21:39.340
It's going to have lots
of knobs and the reason

01:21:39.340 --> 01:21:41.131
it has all those knobs,
is because it knows

01:21:41.131 --> 01:21:42.857
a lot about your business.

01:21:42.857 --> 01:21:43.690
Thank you, you guys.

01:21:43.690 --> 01:21:47.040
[APPLAUSE]

